WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:55.355 --> 00:00:56.238
[music]

00:01:00.297 --> 00:01:03.795
[Ben] This is the Tennessee home
for the incurables.

00:01:03.862 --> 00:01:06.491
I think it opened in like 1906.

00:01:06.558 --> 00:01:12.528
And it was a place for people with terminal
illnesses like tuberculosis, quadriplegics,

00:01:12.595 --> 00:01:16.676
yellow fever, stuff that used to
kill everybody, they would end up here.

00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:19.650
This building is almost like
a metaphor for Memphis.

00:01:19.723 --> 00:01:21.950
You got something here,
the bones are great.

00:01:22.017 --> 00:01:24.283
You can see this place
has a ton of potential.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:28.922
It's just been neglected
and fallen into disrepair for 25 years now.

00:01:29.439 --> 00:01:33.133
[Peter] So we're gonna get into it today,
your guys' stories.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.875
You were on the streets, deep into drugs.

00:01:35.942 --> 00:01:39.884
Came out and now are getting people
off the streets who are into drugs.

00:01:39.951 --> 00:01:43.452
-Bingo, and our story started
right around here

00:01:43.519 --> 00:01:47.807
Your in the epicenter of
where all our madness took place.

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:51.451
In fact we probably both
shoulda died on the street right next door.

00:01:51.518 --> 00:01:53.998
But Jess and I,
the whole time we were out here

00:01:54.065 --> 00:01:55.930
we had this fox hole prayer

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:59.685
that if God would get us out of
hell together we'd come back for everybody

00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:01.871
and so when we came back to Memphis

00:02:02.549 --> 00:02:05.843
we came right back here
and this is where it all started.

00:02:06.308 --> 00:02:09.462
It's taken over by squatters
and the criminal element.

00:02:09.529 --> 00:02:10.723
Lost people.

00:02:11.253 --> 00:02:13.300
You don't just get addicted to the dope.

00:02:13.367 --> 00:02:14.894
You get addicted to the chaos.

00:02:14.961 --> 00:02:18.664
You get addicted to the insanity,
the excitement.

00:02:18.731 --> 00:02:22.515
And so what I see end up happening a lot
is people get clean.

00:02:22.943 --> 00:02:25.542
They'll go get a job,
get back in their kids' lives.

00:02:25.609 --> 00:02:27.080
They're rebuilding,

00:02:27.655 --> 00:02:29.670
and there's no excitement.

00:02:30.565 --> 00:02:31.513
-Right.

00:02:31.585 --> 00:02:34.816
We've baked chaos into what we do.

00:02:34.883 --> 00:02:38.790
That's why we're out here, it's not just
because this is where the addicts are.

00:02:38.857 --> 00:02:40.293
This is where I love to be.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:44.757
And I think for most addicts,
we miss the unpredictability,

00:02:44.824 --> 00:02:46.753
the uncertainty that happens out here,

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:48.434
but we still want to stay clean.

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:51.570
So if you can find a way
to bring people out of addiction,

00:02:51.637 --> 00:02:54.818
get them into recovery,
and right back in the damn hood,

00:02:54.885 --> 00:02:56.680
you check that chaos box.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:59.015
-Totally makes sense.

00:02:59.389 --> 00:03:00.683
You know, on Skid Row,

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:04.856
a lot of those guys
can get the housing if they want to.

00:03:04.923 --> 00:03:07.073
-But you've got to play by the rules.
-Yeah.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.483
And a guy told me
I love the adventure of it.

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:11.850
-Yeah, 100%. Absolutely.
-The adrenaline of it.

00:03:11.917 --> 00:03:16.159
And if you're going into normie society,
like living in the burbs 9:00 to 5:00.

00:03:16.871 --> 00:03:18.971
-Driving a commute every day.
-That sucks.

00:03:19.038 --> 00:03:21.238
-From the adventure.
-Yeah.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:24.340
-I get the adventure out of making videos.
-Right.

00:03:24.407 --> 00:03:26.339
We still get to be in the thick of it.

00:03:26.406 --> 00:03:28.984
We had a couple of Green Berets from

00:03:29.498 --> 00:03:32.701
Fort Campbell over here
three four weeks ago,

00:03:32.768 --> 00:03:35.460
and we had a random gunshot
on one of our blocks.

00:03:35.876 --> 00:03:40.176
This combat medic Green Beret dude
ends up getting to treat a gunshot wound

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:42.018
right here in his backyard.

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:44.730
And stuff like that happens
all the time.

00:03:44.797 --> 00:03:48.079
I don't want to make it sound like
we enjoy it when that happens,

00:03:48.146 --> 00:03:52.361
but that's part of the insanity
that keeps us

00:03:53.155 --> 00:03:54.519
on the right path, I guess.

00:03:54.586 --> 00:03:55.502
Gotcha.

00:03:56.144 --> 00:03:57.723
-How long you guys been clean?

00:03:57.790 --> 00:03:58.871
-Seven years.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.503
We left the streets
almost seven years ago exactly.

00:04:03.147 --> 00:04:05.391
In fact, I'm gonna take you over there.

00:04:05.458 --> 00:04:09.543
My rock bottom happened in two places,
and one of them was right there.

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:11.051
Today, we own that house.

00:04:11.118 --> 00:04:12.956
We own the house next door to it.

00:04:13.023 --> 00:04:16.650
We've got women and kids housed here,
but that used to be the trap house.

00:04:16.717 --> 00:04:21.494
In fact, every bit of violence that
happened, I would say within a mile radius,

00:04:21.561 --> 00:04:24.954
maybe more of that house
was fueled by that house.

00:04:25.021 --> 00:04:26.261
This street right here,

00:04:27.172 --> 00:04:30.256
which looks like a little
quiet hood suburban street,

00:04:30.323 --> 00:04:33.853
used to be the most violent street
in South Memphis.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:37.822
And today, I don't even know the last time
that a violent crime happened there.

00:04:37.889 --> 00:04:41.669
Pretty much the day we took control
of that trap house, the street got calm.

00:04:41.736 --> 00:04:43.246
Kids are coming outside again.

00:04:43.313 --> 00:04:45.434
-Okay, explain a trap house.

00:04:45.501 --> 00:04:49.310
Okay, so a trap house where
narcotics and women are bought and sold.

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:53.788
They are the single source of suffering
for any neighborhood in which they exist.

00:04:53.855 --> 00:04:56.403
Every bit of chaos
emanates from the trap.

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:59.171
All the criminal activity
goes back to the trap.

00:04:59.238 --> 00:05:02.150
It's basically where
the criminal element sets up shop.

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:04.684
-Many trap houses still in Memphis?

00:05:04.751 --> 00:05:06.103
Oh, tons, yeah.

00:05:06.173 --> 00:05:09.513
 It's gotten a lot better since
Memphis State Task Force came here.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:11.992
They've hit a lot of
the more known traps.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:17.088
But the biggest problem you run into
out here is lack of economic opportunity.

00:05:17.155 --> 00:05:21.400
So if I go shut down a trap house,
that economic opportunity still exists.

00:05:21.467 --> 00:05:24.256
It's gonna pop right back up
in a different house.

00:05:24.323 --> 00:05:28.458
-And they're probably just gonna get more
secretive and better with it, right?

00:05:28.525 --> 00:05:30.465
-You would think.
-No?

00:05:31.055 --> 00:05:34.963
Maybe in a different city,
but here in Memphis, not so much, man.

00:05:35.036 --> 00:05:36.539
-Okay.
-They don't change sh*t.

00:05:36.606 --> 00:05:40.983
The names and the addresses change,
but the methods, it all stays the same.

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:43.285
-Can we get out there?
-Oh, absolutely.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:50.409
Back when the street was being run
as a dope track, this was the trap house.

00:05:50.476 --> 00:05:52.685
This is the one
I was just telling you about.

00:05:52.752 --> 00:05:54.123
That was a rooming house.

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:55.826
I used to squat in that one.

00:05:55.893 --> 00:05:58.857
We've since torn the house down.
We just rebuilt that.

00:05:58.924 --> 00:06:00.589
-Oh, nice. Looks good.

00:06:00.656 --> 00:06:01.861
Both of these are ours.

00:06:03.017 --> 00:06:04.953
Hey! How you doing?

00:06:05.832 --> 00:06:07.773
-[woman] My hands are dirty.
-Why?

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:10.030
-Gardening.
-This is Cheyenne.

00:06:10.097 --> 00:06:12.893
I'm doing strawberries,
peas, and potatoes.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:14.822
Who all is home?

00:06:14.889 --> 00:06:16.477
Pretty sure Mama and Rekia.

00:06:17.136 --> 00:06:18.869
Oh, hell yeah.

00:06:19.679 --> 00:06:21.162
This was the trap house.

00:06:21.229 --> 00:06:24.464
Me and Jess have smoked
copious amounts of dope in here.

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:26.581
I've seen people die in this house.

00:06:26.648 --> 00:06:29.576
I had a dude put a pistol to my head
and pull the trigger.

00:06:29.643 --> 00:06:31.357
I don't know why it didn't go off.

00:06:31.424 --> 00:06:34.130
I had a dude try to shoot me
behind this house.

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:36.935
-When you say dope, was it fentanyl? Meth?

00:06:37.002 --> 00:06:38.237
No, crack and heroin.

00:06:39.166 --> 00:06:40.983
So this train track right here,

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:43.489
there's this one
and another one right up there.

00:06:43.556 --> 00:06:46.144
And what used to happen,
you see one stopped?

00:06:46.643 --> 00:06:48.723
The one that hits Southern stops, too.

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:51.058
You'll get boxed in between these two.

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:53.233
And back in the day
when that would happen,

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:55.912
that's when things would get
real violent out here real quick.

00:06:55.979 --> 00:06:56.980
-Real quick.
-No way!

00:06:57.047 --> 00:07:00.555
Because the cops couldn't, there's nobody,
no EMS can't get in.

00:07:00.622 --> 00:07:02.641
So any beef that needed to get squashed

00:07:02.708 --> 00:07:05.772
would usually get squashed
when these trains are both stopped.

00:07:05.839 --> 00:07:07.360
This was a scary place to be.

00:07:07.427 --> 00:07:09.380
It's obviously very different today.

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:13.349
This and Woodward were
the two worst streets in South Memphis.

00:07:13.846 --> 00:07:15.742
You're in the 38106 zip code,

00:07:15.809 --> 00:07:20.013
which historically is one of the most
impoverished zip codes in America.

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:22.568
Violent crime rate here
is through the roof.

00:07:22.635 --> 00:07:26.135
If you're a young Black male living here
2020 to 2023,

00:07:26.599 --> 00:07:30.362
you're five times more likely
to die a violent death than a soldier

00:07:30.429 --> 00:07:32.879
serving in the Global War on Terror
the same year.

00:07:32.946 --> 00:07:34.256
Or actually any year.

00:07:34.323 --> 00:07:37.882
Your risk of death if you're fighting
in combat in Iraq or Afghanistan

00:07:37.949 --> 00:07:39.980
was about 25 in 100,000.

00:07:40.382 --> 00:07:44.936
Young Black males in this zip code,
your risk of death is 130 in 100,000.

00:07:45.003 --> 00:07:48.800
Five times more likely to die
as a civilian in South Memphis

00:07:48.867 --> 00:07:51.291
than you are fighting
the Global War on Terror.

00:07:51.358 --> 00:07:52.436
-That's insane.
-Yeah.

00:07:52.503 --> 00:07:56.455
It's actually, let's remove that from
the equation and just say civilian period.

00:07:56.522 --> 00:08:00.764
Your risk of death in Memphis
to being murdered is about 55 in 100,000.

00:08:01.181 --> 00:08:05.463
Just being a civilian in Memphis
is twice as deadly as fighting in combat.

00:08:06.207 --> 00:08:08.457
-Try to wrap your mind around that one.
-Yeah.

00:08:08.524 --> 00:08:12.250
So the only way is to get out,
is that the story for these kids?

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:15.994
So, you know, you got kids
that are growing up on these streets.

00:08:16.061 --> 00:08:18.522
A lot of them get put in
the game, they call it.

00:08:18.589 --> 00:08:19.472
Yep.

00:08:19.539 --> 00:08:22.827
Selling dope a lot of times
to help mama put food on the table.

00:08:22.894 --> 00:08:27.244
They get incarcerated for the first time,
usually between ages 12 and 15.

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:30.260
And every one of them
down to a man will tell you

00:08:30.327 --> 00:08:32.946
that's what taught them
to become a better criminal.

00:08:33.013 --> 00:08:34.122
What's up, bro?

00:08:35.056 --> 00:08:36.156
How you doing, man?

00:08:37.436 --> 00:08:39.436
-What's up, man?
-What's up, buddy?

00:08:40.184 --> 00:08:41.711
[Peter] How you doing?

00:08:41.778 --> 00:08:43.936
-You good today, man?
-Good to see you, bro.

00:08:44.003 --> 00:08:46.547
Good to see you, man.

00:08:46.614 --> 00:08:48.870
-It's been a minute.
-Peter, nice to meet you.

00:08:48.937 --> 00:08:52.065
-It's good to see you, dude.
-We're making a video, guys.

00:08:52.132 --> 00:08:54.952
You've been here, what, 15 years?
I've been here over 30.

00:08:55.019 --> 00:08:56.077
Oh, no sh*t.

00:08:56.144 --> 00:08:57.652
-You've seen a lot here?
-Yeah.

00:08:57.719 --> 00:08:59.484
-Oh, God.
-A whole lot.

00:08:59.551 --> 00:09:01.123
40 years of this street.

00:09:01.190 --> 00:09:03.081
You've seen murders, what?

00:09:03.148 --> 00:09:07.023
Yeah. There was one guy.
He had just came from the Army.

00:09:07.570 --> 00:09:09.350
Got pissed off at us out here.

00:09:09.417 --> 00:09:12.952
Just came out here and just got to
shooting at us and everything, man.

00:09:13.019 --> 00:09:16.155
He was an old guy, though.
He was in a wheelchair.

00:09:16.222 --> 00:09:18.394
He had one of his legs cut off
or something.

00:09:18.461 --> 00:09:20.576
-He was in a wheelchair with a gun?
-Yeah.

00:09:20.643 --> 00:09:23.788
He used to wheel hisself,
you know, he used to mess with them.

00:09:23.855 --> 00:09:26.990
He used to mess with
the whores that come over.

00:09:27.057 --> 00:09:28.833
Callgirls, they used to come over.

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:31.756
-So that house I showed you
used to be a brothel.

00:09:31.823 --> 00:09:33.262
-Yeah.
-No way.

00:09:33.329 --> 00:09:35.325
Yeah, that used to be a whorehouse.

00:09:35.392 --> 00:09:39.843
So did law enforcement just avoid this zone
back in the day or were they here?

00:09:39.910 --> 00:09:40.890
-MPD wouldn't come.

00:09:40.957 --> 00:09:42.649
Yeah. They never come.

00:09:42.716 --> 00:09:44.933
They'll come,
but they couldn't find nothing.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:47.515
They'll come
but they just wouldn’t hit the brakes.

00:09:47.582 --> 00:09:50.639
And it's a dead end
so they didn't want to come on the street.

00:09:50.706 --> 00:09:52.749
They wouldn't even come down here.

00:09:52.835 --> 00:09:57.664
They said, "God damn it,
they ain't want a better… and all that.

00:09:58.163 --> 00:10:01.083
They ain't want to stop and get…
They couldn't get nothing.

00:10:01.150 --> 00:10:04.743
They couldn't find any drugs.
So they just started taking his equipment.

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:07.106
-How long you been sober, man?
-Seven years.

00:10:08.133 --> 00:10:10.170
-Seven years, man.
-Congratulations, brother.

00:10:10.237 --> 00:10:11.944
Appreciate that. It means a lot.

00:10:12.011 --> 00:10:13.417
Congratulations for real.

00:10:13.484 --> 00:10:16.198
And then there's a lot of them
can't get themselves up.

00:10:16.265 --> 00:10:17.388
-Nah.
-For real.

00:10:17.455 --> 00:10:19.052
They need a lot of help.

00:10:19.119 --> 00:10:22.101
[Peter] What's the drug situation
right now? Getting worse? Better?

00:10:22.168 --> 00:10:24.389
-Worse.
-It's worse.

00:10:24.456 --> 00:10:26.193
You got little kids,

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:29.702
13, 14, 15-year-old kids on it.

00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:32.660
Every neighborhood
in this surrounding area, man.

00:10:32.727 --> 00:10:34.200
-Drugs.
-He's been here for a while.

00:10:34.267 --> 00:10:36.738
-Fentanyl's the main thing, or what?
-Yeah.

00:10:37.170 --> 00:10:39.603
Fentanyl, crack, and heroin.

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:42.383
They can't afford it.
So they just… Yeah.

00:10:42.450 --> 00:10:44.410
I don't even remember
the last time I saw heroin.

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:45.879
50 cent to a dollar.

00:10:45.946 --> 00:10:47.645
So at least 50 at a time.

00:10:47.712 --> 00:10:50.088
-You buying in bulk.
-You buy in bulk, okay.

00:10:50.155 --> 00:10:51.853
Yeah, 50 cent to a dollar.

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:55.802
-So that 50 cent a pill,
how much does that sell for on the street?

00:10:56.675 --> 00:10:58.536
You can get it for five or ten bucks.

00:10:58.603 --> 00:11:00.403
-Yeah, but…
-Just like they sell…

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:02.395
It's like $2.00, $3.00 a piece, man.

00:11:02.462 --> 00:11:06.211
When fent first started coming out here
and they're doing the press 30s,

00:11:06.278 --> 00:11:08.959
our dumb ass were still paying
30 bucks a pill.

00:11:09.026 --> 00:11:11.732
Cause they used to go for
a dollar a milligram.

00:11:12.422 --> 00:11:15.622
Once Fent got out here, it changed
the economics significantly.

00:11:15.689 --> 00:11:18.489
You're not having to deal with
diversion from pharmacies.

00:11:18.556 --> 00:11:22.048
It's being pressed at industrial scale
in labs in Mexico now.

00:11:22.115 --> 00:11:24.582
-It's in everything now.
-Yeah, it's in all of it.

00:11:24.649 --> 00:11:25.599
It's in everything.

00:11:25.666 --> 00:11:27.295
-Okay, what's the point of that?

00:11:27.362 --> 00:11:30.256
From a dealer's standpoint?
Why would you do that?

00:11:30.323 --> 00:11:34.760
If they can make it more addictive,
they're gonna sell more of it.

00:11:34.827 --> 00:11:38.494
And then there's a lot of what happens is
it's accidentally getting mixed.

00:11:38.561 --> 00:11:41.671
If you've ever been in a trap house
where they're serving dope.

00:11:41.738 --> 00:11:44.939
You got White powder in two places,
coke here, fent here.

00:11:45.006 --> 00:11:46.968
It ends up getting accidentally mixed.

00:11:47.035 --> 00:11:50.604
-It's not like Swiss pharmacy standards.
-No, exactly.

00:11:50.912 --> 00:11:55.032
Then they're taking the card
and they scraping it on the same scale.

00:11:55.478 --> 00:11:57.543
And it's so potent.

00:11:57.941 --> 00:12:01.506
It only takes a couple of grains of fent
getting mixed in with your coke

00:12:01.573 --> 00:12:04.020
for you to cook up some crack and hit it.

00:12:04.087 --> 00:12:05.969
And you're done. It's over with.

00:12:08.347 --> 00:12:10.930
So what was the catalyst
for getting into the drugs?

00:12:10.997 --> 00:12:13.666
Did you grow up
like in a super bad family?

00:12:13.733 --> 00:12:15.901
-No.
-You got ostracized?

00:12:15.968 --> 00:12:17.462
My parents are still married.

00:12:17.529 --> 00:12:19.938
They live 10 minutes
up the street in Georgia.

00:12:20.005 --> 00:12:24.003
I had a perfect childhood. My dad was
an Army Ranger. My mom was an engineer.

00:12:24.070 --> 00:12:26.299
She helped build
the Columbia Space Shuttle.

00:12:26.366 --> 00:12:28.066
-No way!
-I was gifted.

00:12:28.133 --> 00:12:29.146
No way.

00:12:29.213 --> 00:12:31.194
We moved to California when I was 13

00:12:31.261 --> 00:12:34.251
and I started struggling with anxiety
really, really bad

00:12:34.318 --> 00:12:35.546
and ADHD.

00:12:35.613 --> 00:12:38.972
Docs put me on Ritalin and that's
really where my addiction started.

00:12:39.039 --> 00:12:40.989
-It was Ritalin, Adderall.
-Interesting.

00:12:41.056 --> 00:12:42.639
That fueled the anxiety.

00:12:42.706 --> 00:12:45.264
-And I discovered alcohol at 13 too.
-Okay.

00:12:45.331 --> 00:12:47.537
Alcohol was always my biggest problem.

00:12:47.604 --> 00:12:51.923
I dabbled in drugs, you know,
all of them when I was 14, 15.

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:54.608
Ended up in residential treatment.

00:12:54.675 --> 00:12:55.913
Got it together.

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:59.686
Went back, finished college,
started a family, did all that.

00:12:59.753 --> 00:13:02.518
And then I got in
a really bad car wreck in 2007

00:13:03.122 --> 00:13:07.023
and started dealing with
recurrent staph infections from it.

00:13:07.465 --> 00:13:10.391
And so I ended up on pain meds.

00:13:10.458 --> 00:13:14.193
And ended up addicted
to the pain meds in 2011, I think.

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:15.173
-Okay.

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:19.323
Well, that progressed and you heard
the conversation we just had.

00:13:19.390 --> 00:13:23.383
You know, a dollar a milligram
for pain pills is pretty d*mned expensive.

00:13:23.450 --> 00:13:25.855
And when you have a tolerance
that adds up quick.

00:13:25.922 --> 00:13:28.815
I ended up with a $600 a day
pain pill habit.

00:13:28.882 --> 00:13:31.489
-Were you on Oxy or what?
-Yeah, yeah. I was on Oxys.

00:13:31.556 --> 00:13:34.529
I ended up hiring a guy
who had a heroin habit.

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:36.854
And he introduced me to heroin.

00:13:36.921 --> 00:13:40.363
And it was, in the beginning,
exponentially cheaper than pain meds.

00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:42.473
And I was like, this is great.
And it feels better.

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:43.921
But that didn't last long.

00:13:43.988 --> 00:13:47.051
within a matter of days
from the first time I shot up,

00:13:47.118 --> 00:13:50.554
what had been a
$300 to $600 a day pain pill habit

00:13:50.621 --> 00:13:53.615
ended up becoming
an $800 a day heroin habit.

00:13:53.682 --> 00:13:54.813
How'd you afford that?

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:56.223
Well, I owned a business.

00:13:56.290 --> 00:13:57.838
And I was doing pretty good.

00:13:58.302 --> 00:14:01.113
-What kind of business?
-It was electronics recycling.

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:05.343
So we were buying truckloads
of freight-damaged flat-screen TVs.

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:06.293
Okay.

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:08.790
We would test and sell
the parts to the industry.

00:14:08.857 --> 00:14:10.807
There were very few of us in the world.

00:14:10.874 --> 00:14:15.363
And you could operate on that level
of intoxication or drug usage?

00:14:15.430 --> 00:14:16.452
-Not really.
-Okay.

00:14:16.519 --> 00:14:19.547
-So that's why I ended up going downhill.
-Okay.

00:14:19.614 --> 00:14:22.311
I think it was 10 months on heroin
that first go around

00:14:22.378 --> 00:14:24.497
before I got arrested for the first time.

00:14:24.564 --> 00:14:27.165
I had two businesses,
the electronics distributorship,

00:14:27.232 --> 00:14:29.434
and then we also had a tactical brand.

00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:32.456
And so I got pulled over
with a bunch of guns.

00:14:32.523 --> 00:14:35.560
And what at the time
I thought was a ton of dope,

00:14:35.627 --> 00:14:37.696
I had like six grams of dope on me.

00:14:37.763 --> 00:14:41.683
And that's next to nothing now
with the work we do today.

00:14:41.750 --> 00:14:44.189
But they charged me
like I was Pablo Escobar.

00:14:44.256 --> 00:14:48.163
So I went from no criminal record,
Cub Scout master, you know,

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:50.790
T-ball coach, business owner, employer,

00:14:50.857 --> 00:14:53.386
to being charged
with 14 felonies overnight.

00:14:53.796 --> 00:14:59.453
Okay. So that must have been brutal because
the community, all the people you knew,

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:01.634
probably were like, what happened to Ben?

00:15:01.701 --> 00:15:04.801
Exactly. We lived out in Lakeland,
which is a suburb.

00:15:04.868 --> 00:15:06.336
We were on a golf course.

00:15:06.403 --> 00:15:08.368
And I was instantly a pariah.

00:15:08.435 --> 00:15:11.559
You know, the news went out there.
I was PNG'd from everywhere.

00:15:11.626 --> 00:15:14.383
But these guys out here
welcomed me back with open arms.

00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:16.078
Of course they did. I had money.

00:15:16.145 --> 00:15:18.343
I ended up basically
moving into this house.

00:15:18.418 --> 00:15:19.895
This was back in 2014.

00:15:19.962 --> 00:15:22.479
The house didn't look
anything like this back then.

00:15:22.546 --> 00:15:25.532
-It looked like maybe that over there?
-Exactly like that.

00:15:25.599 --> 00:15:27.699
-Worse than that actually.
-Like that?

00:15:27.766 --> 00:15:28.961
Like this, yeah.

00:15:29.028 --> 00:15:32.731
So I moved into this house and ended up
living over there with prostitutes.

00:15:32.798 --> 00:15:35.415
-Like how did you end up here?
The drugs brought you?

00:15:35.482 --> 00:15:36.662
Drugs brought me. Yeah.

00:15:36.729 --> 00:15:39.303
So it's just like word on the street
where to go?

00:15:39.370 --> 00:15:40.958
Yeah. Well, it was… [sighs]

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:45.312
Obviously, I was the only White guy
out here, you know?

00:15:45.379 --> 00:15:48.780
And so it was a process of earning trust.

00:15:49.857 --> 00:15:51.542
Lucretia, what's up, girl?

00:15:51.609 --> 00:15:53.732
-Okay, yeah. What was that like?

00:15:53.799 --> 00:15:56.222
Hey, 'Cretia. She can talk about this.

00:15:56.289 --> 00:15:59.425
Lucretia, she's a few years
out of the game herself now.

00:15:59.492 --> 00:16:02.603
She's clean. She lives with her wife,
couple of her grandkids.

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:04.330
Hey, Peter. Nice to meet you.

00:16:04.397 --> 00:16:05.809
Where we meet, 'Cretia?

00:16:06.520 --> 00:16:08.720
On ward.
Your hand coming through the window.

00:16:08.787 --> 00:16:10.468
Snatched some dope off the table.

00:16:10.535 --> 00:16:13.603
-Wait, wait. What was that?
-He snatched dope off the table.

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:15.875
'Cretia used to sell dope and women.

00:16:15.975 --> 00:16:19.420
You have men coming over wanting
to get high, find a woman.

00:16:19.487 --> 00:16:22.825
They'll pay me, I'm going' out there
and find 'em, bring ' back.

00:16:22.892 --> 00:16:26.147
-Okay, you were the middle man.
-Middle man for they money.

00:16:26.214 --> 00:16:28.314
-Long as I get mine.
-It'll blow your mind.

00:16:28.381 --> 00:16:30.788
-20 bucks out here, dude.
-20 bucks!?

00:16:30.855 --> 00:16:33.628
I've seen women turn tricks
for half a pack of Newports.

00:16:33.695 --> 00:16:36.559
-Or a nickel rock.
-Or a $5 rock of crack.

00:16:36.957 --> 00:16:38.652
It's sad. I mean, it is bad.

00:16:38.719 --> 00:16:40.789
Rikaya! Hey, baby!

00:16:41.973 --> 00:16:45.208
We actually fostered her,
well, my business partner did,

00:16:45.591 --> 00:16:48.781
while her mom was getting help.
Her mom's doing great now.

00:16:48.848 --> 00:16:51.973
-Okay, so Ben, just to wrap up on,
you came into this house.

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:54.463
How was it letting
the White guy into town?

00:16:54.530 --> 00:16:56.258
-Suspect.
-Suspect?

00:16:56.325 --> 00:16:59.991
We didn't have White people
just come to the windows or doors.

00:17:00.058 --> 00:17:01.464
We were like, you the po-po.

00:17:01.531 --> 00:17:03.781
You thought he was po-po.
He looks like po-po.

00:17:03.848 --> 00:17:06.730
-I look like po-po.
-Yeah, he came one day bloody.

00:17:07.570 --> 00:17:08.923
-Knocked him out.
-Yeah.

00:17:09.840 --> 00:17:11.428
-Who knocked him out?
-Jess.

00:17:11.910 --> 00:17:13.548
Used to beat the hell out of me.

00:17:13.615 --> 00:17:16.076
Okay, and so you guys.

00:17:16.143 --> 00:17:18.247
And then he hid his door in the car.

00:17:18.314 --> 00:17:21.265
That's how far down we fell, man.
We lived in a f*cking car.

00:17:21.332 --> 00:17:23.243
I had a lot of up and down, man.

00:17:23.310 --> 00:17:25.546
We met after I got arrested
that first time.

00:17:25.613 --> 00:17:28.291
I was court ordered
into Narcotics Anonymous.

00:17:28.358 --> 00:17:29.362
That's where we met.

00:17:29.429 --> 00:17:31.102
We ended up relapsing together

00:17:31.169 --> 00:17:34.248
and lost the house that I had
out on the golf course.

00:17:34.315 --> 00:17:38.193
Got a bigger house.
Got the businesses back off the ground.

00:17:38.260 --> 00:17:41.912
Got a way bigger house and then decided
to start getting high again.

00:17:41.979 --> 00:17:44.539
And that's when
I brought Jess out to South Memphis.

00:17:44.606 --> 00:17:48.103
And so Jess got introduced to this house,
to 'Crecia and her house.

00:17:48.170 --> 00:17:49.876
-How long have you been clean?

00:17:50.310 --> 00:17:51.595
Going on three.

00:17:52.169 --> 00:17:53.542
-Three years?
-Yeah.

00:17:53.609 --> 00:17:55.491
What do you do to fill the void?

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:59.245
Taking care of baby and working every day

00:17:59.312 --> 00:18:01.092
and taking care of the house.

00:18:01.159 --> 00:18:02.191
Looks good.

00:18:02.258 --> 00:18:06.226
She's under selling it. She's brought
a dozen kids through this house.

00:18:06.293 --> 00:18:07.279
They'll foster.

00:18:07.346 --> 00:18:10.669
They've fostered an entire family
the school year before last.

00:18:10.743 --> 00:18:12.458
Mom, dad, three kids.

00:18:12.525 --> 00:18:14.757
We didn't tell them to.
They rent this house.

00:18:14.824 --> 00:18:16.374
They can do whatever they want.

00:18:16.441 --> 00:18:20.028
But they're paying it forward.
Staying in the middle of all the chaos,

00:18:20.095 --> 00:18:21.457
like we were talking about.

00:18:21.531 --> 00:18:24.881
The lady down there run down here
when her little boyfriend beat up.

00:18:24.948 --> 00:18:26.488
-Yep.
-We had her.

00:18:26.555 --> 00:18:28.822
Yeah. This is like, this is a safe house.

00:18:28.890 --> 00:18:30.596
And the one next door is too.

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:33.823
[man] Yes. My lovely girl.

00:18:33.890 --> 00:18:36.599
So this is the first house
Chris renovated.

00:18:38.894 --> 00:18:41.343
We spent $300,000
fixing this house property.

00:18:41.410 --> 00:18:42.584
-No way.
-Yeah.

00:18:42.651 --> 00:18:44.366
I had no idea what I was doing.

00:18:44.433 --> 00:18:47.133
He had just come to us
straight out of custody.

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:49.809
He's done over half his life incarcerated.

00:18:50.280 --> 00:18:53.016
He had no teeth and one eye
when he came to us.

00:18:53.083 --> 00:18:56.156
And this is like my closure house.
I should have died in here.

00:18:56.223 --> 00:18:59.876
My blood was still on the floor
when we came in here the first time.

00:18:59.943 --> 00:19:03.549
My friend's blood was still on the floor
back there, you know.

00:19:03.616 --> 00:19:06.316
And Chris was like,
man, I know how to renovate houses.

00:19:06.383 --> 00:19:08.588
And I was like,
well, let's go figure it out.

00:19:08.655 --> 00:19:10.755
Let's get to work
and see what we get done.

00:19:10.822 --> 00:19:12.351
And here we are, you know.

00:19:13.777 --> 00:19:16.996
[Peter] Okay. So back here
is where you almost got executed?

00:19:17.063 --> 00:19:19.746
-Yeah.
And so many people died out here, man.

00:19:19.813 --> 00:19:21.356
This was hell for a long time.

00:19:21.423 --> 00:19:23.337
The street, we called the street hell.

00:19:23.404 --> 00:19:26.349
That was the door to hell.
The front was barricaded.

00:19:26.416 --> 00:19:28.546
The back door is where we had to go in.

00:19:28.613 --> 00:19:32.213
-So are there still parts of Memphis
that were like what this was before?

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:33.822
100% man. Yeah.

00:19:33.889 --> 00:19:37.857
-We can cruise by some of these today?
Yeah, we'll go. I'm not gonna take you in.

00:19:37.924 --> 00:19:40.462
-Sure, of course.
-'Cause you have a camera.

00:19:40.536 --> 00:19:43.683
They won't like that,
but I'll take you by some traps.

00:19:43.750 --> 00:19:47.756
Some of them are being run by dudes
whose other traps we already shut down.

00:19:47.823 --> 00:19:51.523
Like they helped us shut them down,
but they still got to go make a living.

00:19:51.590 --> 00:19:54.696
-So you're not well liked?
-I'm very well liked by them.

00:19:54.763 --> 00:19:57.319
-The guys that you're shutting down.
-Yeah, they still like us.

00:19:57.386 --> 00:19:58.219
Why?

00:19:58.286 --> 00:20:01.566
Because I've gotten their
baby mama's help, their mom off of dope.

00:20:01.633 --> 00:20:04.100
I've gotten their sister
to stop selling her body.

00:20:04.167 --> 00:20:05.667
They love what we do.

00:20:05.734 --> 00:20:08.501
I'm not trying
to send the dope boys to jail ever.

00:20:08.568 --> 00:20:12.270
In fact, when they get rolled up,
we're working with the district attorney,

00:20:12.337 --> 00:20:14.876
whoever we can
to get them on a better path.

00:20:14.943 --> 00:20:18.043
Because I want to see…
We're not gonna fix the problem out here

00:20:18.110 --> 00:20:20.283
without the dope boys leading the charge.

00:20:20.350 --> 00:20:23.733
I'm, you know, a bald White dude
covered in tattoos.

00:20:23.800 --> 00:20:27.489
The younger generation of Memphis
is not gonna listen to me.

00:20:27.903 --> 00:20:30.091
I need those OGs. I need the dope boys.

00:20:30.158 --> 00:20:33.354
I need them to come back out here
and carry a message of hope

00:20:33.421 --> 00:20:36.821
saying you ain't gotta live this way.
You don't have to go to prison.

00:20:36.907 --> 00:20:39.169
This life doesn't have anything for you.

00:20:39.236 --> 00:20:41.471
Let's do this, this, and this instead.

00:20:42.100 --> 00:20:45.335
So we're trying, like…
The dope boys do love what we do.

00:20:45.990 --> 00:20:48.214
-This is one of
the new houses you've done.

00:20:48.281 --> 00:20:50.513
We call this Sparrow 2.

00:20:50.580 --> 00:20:53.683
These are the houses for women and kids
coming out of human trafficking.

00:20:53.750 --> 00:20:55.154
Chris helped build this one?

00:20:55.221 --> 00:20:58.537
-Chris designed this from the ground up.
-Nice, Chris.

00:20:58.604 --> 00:21:01.005
-And built it.
-That's cool, man. You got skills.

00:21:01.072 --> 00:21:02.193
God is good, bro.

00:21:02.260 --> 00:21:04.263
This is a much nicer one.

00:21:04.330 --> 00:21:08.632
You know, lots of people, when they're
doing a house for people out of addiction

00:21:08.699 --> 00:21:10.966
and, you know,
trafficking women or something,

00:21:11.033 --> 00:21:14.503
they're just kind of trying to
throw something together.

00:21:14.570 --> 00:21:17.671
I didn't want it thrown together.
I wanted something very nice.

00:21:17.738 --> 00:21:20.867
-Yep.
-I wanted clean, nice.

00:21:20.934 --> 00:21:22.842
Something that they could be proud of,

00:21:22.909 --> 00:21:26.153
Something that they could
feel worthy of living in it.

00:21:26.220 --> 00:21:29.846
Because that's important
and a lot of them had a rough childhood.

00:21:29.913 --> 00:21:34.460
I had a rough childhood where I had
cabinets and the doors were hanging off,

00:21:34.527 --> 00:21:36.890
and the counters were peeling.

00:21:37.300 --> 00:21:39.526
And a lot of them
come from houses like that.

00:21:39.605 --> 00:21:43.218
So I thought it was very important
for them to be able to come to a place

00:21:43.285 --> 00:21:44.818
where they could feel good.

00:21:44.891 --> 00:21:47.187
-That's huge, right? Psychologically?
-Yes.

00:21:47.254 --> 00:21:48.887
Having a nice space like this.

00:21:49.320 --> 00:21:51.368
And then, Chris, that's gotta feel good.

00:21:51.435 --> 00:21:54.237
Like, you got to get purpose
in building these things.

00:21:54.304 --> 00:21:57.351
I'll be honest with you, man.
I was strung out all my life.

00:21:57.418 --> 00:21:59.976
And then I said a prayer to God
asking Him for help.

00:22:00.043 --> 00:22:03.583
And that's when He put
Ben and Jess in my life.

00:22:03.656 --> 00:22:06.828
And they never looked at my past, man.

00:22:06.895 --> 00:22:09.513
They just gave me a chance.
I get purpose out of it.

00:22:09.580 --> 00:22:12.493
Now I strive to help others
as well as myself.

00:22:12.560 --> 00:22:15.441
So I do a lot of the work myself
because it saves money.

00:22:15.508 --> 00:22:18.931
'Cause when you have subcontractors,
they want top dollar.

00:22:18.998 --> 00:22:21.260
Okay, so the first one was $300,000.

00:22:21.327 --> 00:22:23.504
How much was this one?
What can you get?

00:22:23.571 --> 00:22:26.149
This house here is like $130,000, $140,000.

00:22:26.216 --> 00:22:28.316
But the difference is
he was trying to help

00:22:28.383 --> 00:22:30.933
everybody in the halfway house have a job.

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:34.246
Tying to teach them trades
and careers in doing this house.

00:22:34.313 --> 00:22:36.713
And they didn't know anything
about construction.

00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:40.027
So he was out of a bunch of money
from pocket because of that.

00:22:40.094 --> 00:22:44.496
-The way I have to look at this is,
like, my product is not housing.

00:22:44.563 --> 00:22:46.625
My product is human lives. You know?

00:22:47.286 --> 00:22:49.456
And you look at
these two dudes right here.

00:22:49.523 --> 00:22:51.638
Worth every penny of $300,000.

00:22:51.705 --> 00:22:54.326
And they're not the only two
that benefited from it.

00:22:54.393 --> 00:22:57.063
That changed Chris's life.
It changed Tammy's life.

00:22:57.130 --> 00:22:59.000
It gave Rekia a place to live.

00:22:59.713 --> 00:23:02.181
You know, you can't put dollars on that.

00:23:05.882 --> 00:23:08.373
None of this new construction is happening.

00:23:08.446 --> 00:23:09.961
And it just goes to show, man,

00:23:10.028 --> 00:23:12.971
all it takes to change
the dynamic on one street

00:23:13.038 --> 00:23:15.506
is one person giving a f*ck. That's it.

00:23:16.090 --> 00:23:17.193
Oh, Lord!

00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:19.006
-That's what you're talking about.

00:23:19.073 --> 00:23:21.663
Yeah.
And there's another train track behind us.

00:23:21.730 --> 00:23:24.491
Okay.
So back in the day, does it still happen?

00:23:24.558 --> 00:23:28.103
-This dynamic of the trains?
-No. They will still knock cars off.

00:23:28.170 --> 00:23:31.046
So I used to live in this house,
1420 East McLemore.

00:23:31.113 --> 00:23:32.726
That garage behind it,

00:23:32.793 --> 00:23:36.156
They'll knock off train cars
and go hide merchandise in that garage.

00:23:36.223 --> 00:23:37.123
-Seriously?
-Yeah.

00:23:37.190 --> 00:23:39.362
I haven't seen it happen in about a year.

00:23:39.430 --> 00:23:41.254
-[Jess] Yeah.
-But they used to.

00:23:42.723 --> 00:23:44.723
This is Shady Lane that were on now

00:23:45.086 --> 00:23:47.997
and everything about this street is shady.

00:23:48.773 --> 00:23:51.331
-Used to be shady.
-It looks pretty good.

00:23:51.398 --> 00:23:54.548
-These are cool homes actually.
-There's a really cool one here.

00:23:54.615 --> 00:23:56.713
I want this one actually… bad.

00:23:56.780 --> 00:24:00.096
-Right, nice old brick homes,
steep roofs.

00:24:00.490 --> 00:24:02.421
That one's new or redone.

00:24:03.480 --> 00:24:06.444
For deep hood, this actually
looks pretty good, I gotta say.

00:24:06.511 --> 00:24:10.983
Right. So you're in one of
the more historic areas right now.

00:24:11.050 --> 00:24:11.982
-Okay.

00:24:12.049 --> 00:24:15.053
One statistic I meant to give you,
this is very important.

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:18.118
Young men out here
are more likely to be dead or incarcerated

00:24:18.185 --> 00:24:20.156
than to have a job or be in school.

00:24:20.230 --> 00:24:23.288
-That single data point right there is…
-Tells you everything.

00:24:23.355 --> 00:24:24.430
Yeah, exactly.

00:24:24.497 --> 00:24:28.033
If we could change that you could change
the whole dynamic out here.

00:24:28.100 --> 00:24:30.256
Oh, my God. These trains are killing me.

00:24:30.323 --> 00:24:33.373
-That's a cool train though.
-Oh, my God. Bro, it's tanks!

00:24:35.790 --> 00:24:37.049
[Ben] What in the hell?

00:24:45.340 --> 00:24:49.002
When you're on the ground talking
to everyday people behind the headlines,

00:24:49.069 --> 00:24:53.443
you realize how different reality can be
from what the media delivers us.

00:24:53.510 --> 00:24:57.136
The same story can be spun
in completely different ways.

00:24:57.203 --> 00:24:59.033
That's why I use Ground News.

00:24:59.100 --> 00:25:01.833
You pull in thousands of articles
from around the world

00:25:01.900 --> 00:25:03.603
and organize them by story

00:25:03.670 --> 00:25:07.503
so you can see how the left, center,
and right are covering the same event.

00:25:07.570 --> 00:25:11.213
Their color-coded layout shows
each outlet's political bias.

00:25:11.280 --> 00:25:14.283
Take this same story about
the Memphis Safe Task Force.

00:25:14.350 --> 00:25:16.678
A right-leaning outlet
leads with the headline,

00:25:16.745 --> 00:25:18.734
Lawsuit hits Trump's Memphis Task Force

00:25:18.801 --> 00:25:23.396
as marshals reveal 9,000 arrests
and 1,500 guns seized.

00:25:23.463 --> 00:25:25.725
While a left-leaning journal
frames the story,

00:25:25.792 --> 00:25:28.655
Memphis residents
claim harassment, arrest, and abuse

00:25:28.722 --> 00:25:31.463
by Trump-ordered Memphis Safe Task Force.

00:25:31.530 --> 00:25:35.063
Two completely different headlines
pertaining to the same story.

00:25:35.130 --> 00:25:39.873
Ground News puts the coverage side-by-side
so we can spot the bias ourselves.

00:25:39.940 --> 00:25:43.739
Their Blindspot feed also shows stories
one side is covering heavily

00:25:43.806 --> 00:25:45.716
while the other barely touches them.

00:25:45.783 --> 00:25:48.483
Ground News is fully independent
and reader-supported.

00:25:48.550 --> 00:25:53.483
I partnered with them to get my viewers
