Subscribe to Ground News.
Support my reporting.

00:26:03.195 --> 00:26:05.443
And take control of how you see the world.

00:26:05.510 --> 00:26:06.876
Now back to the story.

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:10.586
[Peter] Midday like today,
bullets aren't flying.

00:26:10.653 --> 00:26:11.433
Oh, they are.

00:26:11.500 --> 00:26:14.501
It's a lot better now
because of the Memphis Safe Task Force.

00:26:14.568 --> 00:26:17.579
We've actually gone a week out here
without a shooting.

00:26:17.980 --> 00:26:20.651
Generally, you'll have a shooting
down here every day.

00:26:20.718 --> 00:26:24.049
In fact, not long ago,
we had three within a quarter mile

00:26:24.116 --> 00:26:26.113
of this intersection in one day.

00:26:26.180 --> 00:26:28.209
The Parkway Inn is a ho motel.

00:26:28.277 --> 00:26:30.350
-Down there?
-Yeah, and then there's…

00:26:30.417 --> 00:26:31.997
You'll see here in a second.

00:26:32.076 --> 00:26:36.076
There's Sun Inn and Skyway Inn,
Bellevue Inn right up there.

00:26:36.143 --> 00:26:39.423
These are all hourly motels
where women go to turn tricks.

00:26:39.490 --> 00:26:41.784
The new pavement is kind of misleading.

00:26:42.200 --> 00:26:44.580
But, you know,
there's some science behind that.

00:26:44.647 --> 00:26:47.706
If your hood looks nicer,
you're gonna take better care of it.

00:26:47.773 --> 00:26:50.609
-Broken glass theory, right?
-Yeah, absolutely.

00:26:50.676 --> 00:26:52.454
And so I do buy into that.

00:26:52.521 --> 00:26:54.971
And more than anecdotally.
We've seen it play out.

00:26:55.038 --> 00:26:57.215
Matter of fact,
where I'm about to take you,

00:26:57.282 --> 00:26:59.601
you're gonna see
broken glass theory in action.

00:26:59.668 --> 00:27:01.468
All right, so this is Elvis Presley.

00:27:01.535 --> 00:27:03.623
That was South Parkway we just turned off.

00:27:03.690 --> 00:27:05.686
This is my building right here.

00:27:05.753 --> 00:27:07.482
You see my We Fight Monsters sign?

00:27:07.549 --> 00:27:08.784
Oh, right here. Nice.

00:27:09.340 --> 00:27:11.328
Guys, we're gonna get more into that.

00:27:11.395 --> 00:27:13.820
But just want to let you know,
We Fight Monsters.

00:27:13.887 --> 00:27:16.231
That's what Ben and Jess are doing.

00:27:16.298 --> 00:27:17.932
That's their organization.

00:27:17.999 --> 00:27:20.900
Getting people off the streets
into homes like we've seen.

00:27:20.967 --> 00:27:24.185
And that's not all you do.
That's just like your passion project.

00:27:24.252 --> 00:27:26.913
-You also have a business, right?
-I've got several.

00:27:26.980 --> 00:27:29.574
This is the most expensive hobby
in the world.

00:27:29.641 --> 00:27:31.745
-Okay, okay.
-But here's the thing, man.

00:27:31.812 --> 00:27:34.141
It takes all of this for us to stay clean.

00:27:34.208 --> 00:27:37.982
We've tried building businesses
and raising a family.

00:27:38.049 --> 00:27:39.732
And we relapsed every time.

00:27:39.799 --> 00:27:42.944
We've rebuilt life together
and burned it to the ground twice.

00:27:43.011 --> 00:27:45.335
And so this time
we decided to do it different.

00:27:45.402 --> 00:27:47.924
And we started the nonprofit
with our excess.

00:27:48.395 --> 00:27:50.370
So this is Wilbur Street. All right.

00:27:50.830 --> 00:27:53.301
That is the house
where we hit rock bottom.

00:27:54.748 --> 00:28:00.035
So I woke up in an empty lot next door
to the trap house in South Memphis,

00:28:00.102 --> 00:28:02.446
covered in blood
and had no idea what happened..

00:28:02.513 --> 00:28:05.101
This is that empty lot.
It's no longer empty.

00:28:05.580 --> 00:28:07.253
And this is that trap house.

00:28:07.320 --> 00:28:10.502
This was the first house
that we came back to Memphis

00:28:11.280 --> 00:28:13.010
with the intent of shutting down.

00:28:13.077 --> 00:28:15.242
It was an active dope house at the time.

00:28:15.309 --> 00:28:17.390
This is the window
Lucretia told you about

00:28:17.457 --> 00:28:20.281
that my hand went through
and stole all her dope.

00:28:20.900 --> 00:28:23.972
They used to do hand-to-hand transactions
through this window.

00:28:24.039 --> 00:28:26.989
I have no idea how many people
have been shot in this house.

00:28:27.056 --> 00:28:30.201
You can see the gunshots coming back out
because we'd be shooting back.

00:28:30.268 --> 00:28:31.101
-Oh right.

00:28:31.168 --> 00:28:33.331
We've patched a lot in the house itself

00:28:33.944 --> 00:28:35.179
when we renovated it.

00:28:35.580 --> 00:28:37.280
Right now the house is boarded up.

00:28:37.347 --> 00:28:40.778
We had a couple in here that relapsed

00:28:40.845 --> 00:28:42.604
and trashed everything.

00:28:43.140 --> 00:28:45.991
And so since they were buying
dope and all that,

00:28:46.058 --> 00:28:48.959
we decided we're gonna
board this house up for a little bit

00:28:49.026 --> 00:28:51.261
and just let the block calm back down.

00:28:51.793 --> 00:28:55.709
You can tell from the construction
this street is still very calm.

00:28:56.085 --> 00:28:58.073
And that's crazy for where we're at.

00:28:58.140 --> 00:28:59.700
Woodward Street is…

00:29:00.417 --> 00:29:01.573
it was insane.

00:29:01.640 --> 00:29:04.546
I've gone to jail in this spot
more times than I can count.

00:29:04.613 --> 00:29:08.545
My buddy was shot in the head five times
right there, died on the ground.

00:29:08.612 --> 00:29:12.037
Another one of my friends
went missing from that house.

00:29:12.104 --> 00:29:13.738
She was found up the road.

00:29:13.805 --> 00:29:16.158
Her mutilated body left in the cemetery.

00:29:16.790 --> 00:29:19.912
Lucretia's brother's been shot
six times in this house.

00:29:19.979 --> 00:29:22.983
Four times on one occasion,
two times on another occasion.

00:29:23.050 --> 00:29:25.600
Jess and I have been
held at gunpoint in this house.

00:29:25.670 --> 00:29:27.875
Removed from this house at gunpoint.

00:29:27.942 --> 00:29:29.717
-Recidivism rate is high?

00:29:30.126 --> 00:29:31.267
Staggering.

00:29:31.334 --> 00:29:32.981
-Like what percent?
-90 plus.

00:29:33.537 --> 00:29:36.636
90 plus. Depending on the
demographic you're talking about.

00:29:36.703 --> 00:29:38.759
The recidivism rate is staggeringly high.

00:29:38.826 --> 00:29:41.469
And the reason for that is

00:29:41.536 --> 00:29:45.913
the issues that have people committing
crimes aren't getting addressed.

00:29:45.980 --> 00:29:49.338
You're talking generational trauma,
generational cycles of poverty.

00:29:49.405 --> 00:29:50.561
I'm not making excuses.

00:29:50.628 --> 00:29:54.675
We make the decisions
we make that land us in jail.

00:29:55.310 --> 00:29:58.288
But, you know, I wish programs like

00:29:58.355 --> 00:30:01.644
the Shelby County Drug Court,
Recovery Court,

00:30:01.711 --> 00:30:03.973
Veterans Court, Mental Health Court…

00:30:04.040 --> 00:30:08.147
I wish those got
as much funding as our jails get.

00:30:08.700 --> 00:30:11.900
Because if you're talking about this
from a taxpayer perspective,

00:30:11.967 --> 00:30:15.507
Like, let's say Cody,
who you haven't met yet, stole my car.

00:30:15.574 --> 00:30:18.595
That's what he was.
He was a professional car thief. Alright?

00:30:18.662 --> 00:30:19.795
He steals my car.

00:30:19.862 --> 00:30:22.065
Auto Theft Task Force
builds a case on him.

00:30:22.132 --> 00:30:25.032
They arrest Cody. He goes to jail.
My car has been chopped.

00:30:25.099 --> 00:30:27.983
I'm never getting that back.
I didn't have insurance.

00:30:28.050 --> 00:30:30.423
So it's not like
I'm getting cashed out on that.

00:30:30.490 --> 00:30:32.494
Now, as a taxpayer of Shelby County,

00:30:32.561 --> 00:30:35.821
I'm footing the bill at 45 grand a year
to keep Cody incarcerated.

00:30:35.888 --> 00:30:37.246
I've never been made whole.

00:30:37.313 --> 00:30:40.875
So I'm essentially being re-victimized
by him having to be incarcerated.

00:30:40.942 --> 00:30:45.233
Whereas today, Cody is five years clean
and is paying taxes,

00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:48.380
running a business himself,
managing our men's housing.

00:30:48.810 --> 00:30:50.366
And that's because of re-entry.

00:30:50.433 --> 00:30:55.024
That's because of, you know, focusing
on the actual causes of the problems

00:30:55.091 --> 00:30:56.620
rather than just punitive.

00:30:57.313 --> 00:31:00.435
Right?
And some people belong in institutions.

00:31:00.890 --> 00:31:01.923
Don't dispute that.

00:31:01.990 --> 00:31:04.530
Child molesters,
people that commit sexual violence,

00:31:04.597 --> 00:31:06.343
exceptionally violent individuals.

00:31:06.410 --> 00:31:08.829
These are the people jails are made for.

00:31:08.896 --> 00:31:12.946
There are certain people that have to be
removed from society. You can't fix them.

00:31:13.013 --> 00:31:15.961
I firmly believe child molesters
and people that commit sexual violence

00:31:16.028 --> 00:31:17.166
are in that category.

00:31:17.233 --> 00:31:21.183
The majority of everybody else,
people that commit property crimes,

00:31:21.250 --> 00:31:22.777
a random violent crime,

00:31:23.696 --> 00:31:25.419
and drug crimes in particular.

00:31:25.486 --> 00:31:27.798
Those people, more often than not,
can be fixed

00:31:27.865 --> 00:31:31.265
and can be sent right back out
into these communities to change them.

00:31:31.332 --> 00:31:33.159
-Okay, let me ask you this question.

00:31:33.226 --> 00:31:35.931
My wife and I left San Francisco in '21.

00:31:36.360 --> 00:31:39.769
That's when we had a weak DA
that said the criminals are the victims.

00:31:39.836 --> 00:31:41.643
You could steal up to $950.

00:31:41.710 --> 00:31:44.068
And so there was no stick.

00:31:44.135 --> 00:31:45.896
There was plenty of incentive.

00:31:45.963 --> 00:31:48.943
-And so we just got more of that.
-Yep, 100%.

00:31:49.010 --> 00:31:52.624
So with what you're saying,
recidivism is super high.

00:31:53.137 --> 00:31:55.887
You're saying we just gotta
go with a different approach

00:31:55.954 --> 00:32:00.738
because if there is no consequence,
then it seems like it multiplies.

00:32:00.805 --> 00:32:02.698
-Yes, let me clarify that.
-Yeah.

00:32:02.773 --> 00:32:05.800
I am a law and order guy.
I am not saying no consequence.

00:32:05.867 --> 00:32:09.791
I would not have gotten clean
if I wasn't getting charged

00:32:09.858 --> 00:32:12.571
every other weekend
for possession of narcotics.

00:32:12.638 --> 00:32:15.583
Like gonna jail sucks.
It helped me get clean.

00:32:15.650 --> 00:32:18.818
So getting booked in jail,
you know, having to do 30 days,

00:32:18.885 --> 00:32:21.429
having to do three months,
makes perfect sense.

00:32:21.496 --> 00:32:23.682
We need that. We need those consequences.

00:32:23.749 --> 00:32:25.997
What I'm saying doesn't make sense is like

00:32:26.064 --> 00:32:28.829
when I got arrested
with all the guns and drugs,

00:32:29.422 --> 00:32:31.393
I was looking at 20 years in prison.

00:32:31.460 --> 00:32:33.291
How is that fixing anything, right?

00:32:33.358 --> 00:32:34.756
I needed drug court.

00:32:35.500 --> 00:32:38.429
Now I say that and I still
got myself kicked off drug court

00:32:38.496 --> 00:32:41.194
because I wasn't ready
to get clean, you know.

00:32:41.261 --> 00:32:44.431
But I watch people every single day
come off these same streets,

00:32:44.511 --> 00:32:46.873
end up in our drug court house
and do it right.

00:32:46.945 --> 00:32:50.483
And so the point I was making is
that we need to focus more

00:32:51.243 --> 00:32:55.391
on outcomes-based judicial process

00:32:55.913 --> 00:32:58.013
where there's data-backed stuff behind it.

00:32:58.080 --> 00:33:00.707
-Right.
-Not decriminalization. God, never.

00:33:00.774 --> 00:33:03.803
Like get out of prison
and don't just release like there's…

00:33:03.870 --> 00:33:06.474
-You have to go through a reentry process.
-Yeah.

00:33:06.541 --> 00:33:09.491
And so to that end,
we've met with the US Attorney's Office,

00:33:09.558 --> 00:33:12.291
the Federal Reentry Council,
Bureau of Prisons.

00:33:12.358 --> 00:33:14.517
We work with the drug court
every single day.

00:33:14.584 --> 00:33:17.756
We work with probation
and parole because these systems in place

00:33:17.823 --> 00:33:20.933
is how you prevent
that revolving door from continuing.

00:33:21.430 --> 00:33:22.263
This house,

00:33:23.000 --> 00:33:25.583
this was mine and Jessica's
personal hell.

00:33:25.650 --> 00:33:29.050
And it doesn't look like it today
because we gutted the motherf*cker.

00:33:29.117 --> 00:33:31.273
-This was your personal hell?
-This was our personal hell.

00:33:31.340 --> 00:33:34.069
The number of hours I spent

00:33:35.280 --> 00:33:37.221
in this room wishing I could die.

00:33:39.938 --> 00:33:41.409
Yeah, this house is hell.

00:33:42.424 --> 00:33:44.163
It's so surreal to be in here.

00:33:44.230 --> 00:33:45.063
Yeah.

00:33:45.510 --> 00:33:49.911
-So the only way
you stay from going back into this…

00:33:50.484 --> 00:33:52.381
-…is doing what you're doing right now.
-Yeah.

00:33:52.448 --> 00:33:55.061
'Cause you see it from
a sober perspective firsthand.

00:33:55.128 --> 00:33:57.678
You're like, that looks terrible.
I don't want that.

00:33:57.745 --> 00:34:00.333
-And then you're helping people.
-Yeah. It's…

00:34:02.080 --> 00:34:04.563
I'm a little extra in everything we do.

00:34:04.630 --> 00:34:08.703
And so I don't think everybody
needs to go buy the f*cking trap house

00:34:08.770 --> 00:34:10.726
where they wish they died to get clean.

00:34:10.793 --> 00:34:13.205
But I had to do that. You know, I had to.

00:34:14.280 --> 00:34:17.612
I had to try to get the dudes
that used to sell me dope

00:34:17.679 --> 00:34:20.209
on a better path
because I knew they were good men.

00:34:20.276 --> 00:34:21.193
Lucretia.

00:34:21.260 --> 00:34:23.939
I wasn't wrong about her.
She's kept families together

00:34:24.006 --> 00:34:27.268
'cause I got her and her sister
and her brother out of this house.

00:34:27.335 --> 00:34:30.273
And on paper, that's a narco trafficker.

00:34:30.340 --> 00:34:33.222
That's a human trafficker.
That's a violent felon.

00:34:34.645 --> 00:34:37.731
On paper, there's no logical reason

00:34:37.798 --> 00:34:40.493
she should be doing
what she's doing today.

00:34:40.560 --> 00:34:43.205
Yet there's at least a dozen kids

00:34:43.272 --> 00:34:46.077
who have parents
in their lives because of her.

00:34:46.592 --> 00:34:49.960
And so it takes people
who have actually lived it.

00:34:50.027 --> 00:34:51.892
You can't just institutionalize

00:34:51.959 --> 00:34:55.018
and put programming in place
that says we're gonna do X.

00:34:55.085 --> 00:34:56.428
You have to know these people.

00:34:56.495 --> 00:34:58.301
You have to actually have lived it.

00:34:58.368 --> 00:35:01.011
You have to have bled
in the trenches with them

00:35:01.079 --> 00:35:04.182
to be able to guess who's going to do
what you want them to do.

00:35:04.249 --> 00:35:06.102
You're not always gonna get it right.

00:35:06.169 --> 00:35:08.926
Two of the guys
that used to sell dope out of this house

00:35:08.993 --> 00:35:13.685
that we helped avoid some consequences
and mentored onto a better path.

00:35:13.752 --> 00:35:16.361
One of them has since shot somebody

00:35:16.428 --> 00:35:19.628
and the other got caught
with five and a half pounds of fentanyl.

00:35:19.695 --> 00:35:23.519
You're not always gonna get it right.
But you get 'Creita, Jordan, and Daphne

00:35:23.586 --> 00:35:24.896
who are kicking ass today.

00:35:24.963 --> 00:35:28.763
And then you've got us. We came out of
the same house. And here we are today.

00:35:28.830 --> 00:35:32.293
This house has been used
to put a family back together.

00:35:33.110 --> 00:35:37.723
Explain to those that don't do
meth and fent, what's so great?

00:35:37.790 --> 00:35:40.133
All it seems like
is terrible from the outside.

00:35:40.200 --> 00:35:41.581
What's the draw to that?

00:35:41.648 --> 00:35:44.177
Is when you're in it,
it feels just amazing?

00:35:44.710 --> 00:35:47.023
No, it was miserable. You just can't stop.

00:35:47.090 --> 00:35:48.796
Well, at first it is amazing.

00:35:49.217 --> 00:35:51.183
-That's what kinda brings you in.
-Yeah.

00:35:51.250 --> 00:35:53.301
Addiction begins with a choice.

00:35:53.368 --> 00:35:56.000
You know, that first time,
you made a choice to do it

00:35:56.067 --> 00:35:57.226
and it does feel great.

00:35:57.293 --> 00:35:59.675
But the problem is,
especially with crack,

00:35:59.742 --> 00:36:03.492
you'll spend the rest of your life
chasing that high you had the first time.

00:36:03.559 --> 00:36:05.365
And you'll never get it again. Ever.

00:36:05.432 --> 00:36:08.591
But you are incapable
of not continuing to chase it.

00:36:09.230 --> 00:36:11.012
With opiates, it's different.

00:36:11.079 --> 00:36:15.711
Now, fentanyl came into this house
in early 2018.

00:36:16.370 --> 00:36:18.008
This block in 2018.

00:36:18.075 --> 00:36:21.298
And, you know, Jess and I
both come from a healthcare background.

00:36:21.365 --> 00:36:25.726
My first job after the Army in college
was selling drugs for Pfizer.

00:36:25.806 --> 00:36:28.245
-She was a medical assistant.
-No way.

00:36:28.312 --> 00:36:31.356
We both vehemently
wanted to avoid fentanyl.

00:36:31.423 --> 00:36:33.823
We were afraid of it.
We were addicted to heroin.

00:36:33.890 --> 00:36:35.940
-'Cause you knew what it was?
-Absolutely.

00:36:36.007 --> 00:36:38.222
And, you know,
you're asking what's the draw.

00:36:38.289 --> 00:36:40.823
With heroin, first time you shoot up,

00:36:40.890 --> 00:36:42.751
it feels like God's giving you a hug.

00:36:42.818 --> 00:36:45.473
-It's the most comforting thing on earth.
-Oh, okay.

00:36:45.540 --> 00:36:47.453
All your problems go away?

00:36:47.520 --> 00:36:49.538
The song Comfortably Numb by Pink Floyd?

00:36:49.605 --> 00:36:52.343
That's about heroin.
That's exactly what it is.

00:36:53.152 --> 00:36:56.229
[Peter] Okay,
so who's buying these places?

00:36:56.303 --> 00:36:57.865
[Ben] People in this community.

00:36:57.932 --> 00:37:00.640
That's the goal, I think, for all of us

00:37:00.707 --> 00:37:03.578
is to make this community better
for the people that are already here.

00:37:03.645 --> 00:37:05.758
Nobody's trying to gentrify South Memphis.

00:37:05.825 --> 00:37:07.712
Like, one, it's not gonna work.

00:37:07.779 --> 00:37:10.833
-No college students are coming in here?
-100%.

00:37:10.900 --> 00:37:14.912
Making this place actually better for the
people that live here and grow up here,

00:37:14.979 --> 00:37:17.249
that's something everybody can get behind.

00:37:17.316 --> 00:37:22.618
You've seen already, even though we're in
one of the deadliest zip codes in America,

00:37:22.685 --> 00:37:24.208
everybody's pretty d*mn cool.

00:37:24.275 --> 00:37:25.255
-Yeah, right.

00:37:25.322 --> 00:37:28.952
-So that was broken glass theory
kinda in action right there.

00:37:29.019 --> 00:37:31.901
One person shows up
making it a little bit better.

00:37:32.479 --> 00:37:35.245
-You want the windows up?
-So they don't see the camera.

00:37:35.312 --> 00:37:38.612
They're gonna get weirded out
like a motherf*cker if they see that.

00:37:38.679 --> 00:37:42.174
They're not used to me bringing
other White folks out here to film.

00:37:42.241 --> 00:37:43.140
-All right.

00:37:43.207 --> 00:37:45.246
-So this is a very active dope track.

00:37:45.313 --> 00:37:47.013
I'm not gonna point out the houses

00:37:47.080 --> 00:37:49.438
because I'm not trying
to get anybody rolled up.

00:37:49.505 --> 00:37:51.473
We're still working with
the dope boys out here.

00:37:51.540 --> 00:37:54.260
I've got two of them
that run houses out here,

00:37:54.327 --> 00:37:57.414
have active ongoing cases
in criminal court.

00:37:58.260 --> 00:38:00.643
I would like to see them
get on a better path.

00:38:00.710 --> 00:38:03.260
One of them, I don't think
there's a chance in hell.

00:38:03.327 --> 00:38:06.527
He's had ample opportunity
and he probably needs to be in prison.

00:38:06.594 --> 00:38:08.748
You never know.
They surprise you sometimes.

00:38:08.815 --> 00:38:11.193
-Okay, so you're a kid growing up here.

00:38:11.260 --> 00:38:13.783
What's your life looking like
for the most part?

00:38:13.850 --> 00:38:15.734
You're selling weed by the time you're 12.

00:38:15.801 --> 00:38:16.853
Like best case.

00:38:16.920 --> 00:38:19.193
And it's to help mama
put bread on the table.

00:38:19.260 --> 00:38:21.013
- Yeah, but can't she get welfare?

00:38:21.086 --> 00:38:23.586
Oh, they're getting all that.
But here's the thing.

00:38:23.653 --> 00:38:26.073
When mom's addicted to fent or crack,

00:38:26.140 --> 00:38:28.640
she's selling her food stamps
the day she gets them

00:38:28.707 --> 00:38:30.496
for 50 cents on the dollar.

00:38:30.563 --> 00:38:32.825
There's a lot of stores
around here you can go

00:38:32.892 --> 00:38:36.443
and they'll buy your stamps.
It's illegal but they do it.

00:38:36.510 --> 00:38:41.226
It's also a lot more expensive to be poor
than it is to be just above poor.

00:38:41.293 --> 00:38:43.181
When you're having to rent a room

00:38:43.248 --> 00:38:46.930
and you're in a food desert
there's not healthy food options.

00:38:46.997 --> 00:38:49.663
It's Zoo Zoos and Wham Whams.
Nobody's got cars.

00:38:49.730 --> 00:38:52.433
It's just a very difficult cycle
to get out of.

00:38:52.500 --> 00:38:55.396
And I can say that
because I've been stuck in it myself.

00:38:55.463 --> 00:38:58.997
The food desert argument here would be
why do you want to set up a shop

00:38:59.064 --> 00:39:01.444
when you're gonna have
so much robbery going on?

00:39:01.511 --> 00:39:02.464
-Bro.
-Right?

00:39:02.531 --> 00:39:06.485
That right there, where it's
DG Market now, it was a Walgreen’s.

00:39:06.552 --> 00:39:07.924
Then it was something else.

00:39:07.991 --> 00:39:10.451
How do you put a Walgreen’s
out of business, man?

00:39:10.518 --> 00:39:12.646
And it's shoplifting.

00:39:12.713 --> 00:39:15.541
-Is it the Indian markets that come in?
Is that about it?

00:39:16.891 --> 00:39:18.800
Those don't even last.

00:39:18.867 --> 00:39:21.048
See you'll have an Indian corner store

00:39:21.115 --> 00:39:23.938
or a lot of them
are Iranian run now I think.

00:39:24.005 --> 00:39:26.244
-Those guys have got courage, huh?
-They do.

00:39:26.311 --> 00:39:29.544
And by and large,
they're cool as sh*t too.

00:39:29.611 --> 00:39:32.373
-I love those guys. They have fun.
-Right.

00:39:32.440 --> 00:39:34.102
I have an international audience.

00:39:34.169 --> 00:39:36.933
People are gonna see this
from other parts of the world.

00:39:37.000 --> 00:39:39.659
-And be like, yeah, that's not that bad.
-[Ben laughs]

00:39:39.726 --> 00:39:40.770
-Right?
-Right.

00:39:40.837 --> 00:39:44.398
It's a little shabby, but you got
sidewalks and the pavement isn't dirt.

00:39:44.465 --> 00:39:48.006
And, you know, these houses
just need paint and a little love.

00:39:48.073 --> 00:39:52.873
-Yep. Well, on paper, last year,
this was 400% deadlier

00:39:52.940 --> 00:39:54.823
for a civilian than Ukraine.

00:39:55.191 --> 00:39:58.112
500% deadlier than Iraq or Afghanistan.

00:39:58.510 --> 00:40:00.569
It doesn't look like it, but it is.

00:40:00.970 --> 00:40:04.799
Jess and I used to live in a $700 truck

00:40:04.866 --> 00:40:07.454
in this lot right here
with our two-year-old.

00:40:08.107 --> 00:40:09.176
-Oh, my God.

00:40:11.470 --> 00:40:13.773
When was this nice? 50s?

00:40:13.840 --> 00:40:17.152
-Yeah, probably the 50s.
Most of these homes were built in the 30s.

00:40:17.219 --> 00:40:19.771
And so I would say
it was definitely nice back then,

00:40:19.838 --> 00:40:22.803
but it's just steadily declined ever since.

00:40:23.476 --> 00:40:26.814
-And to be fair, we're showing
just the hood right now today.

00:40:26.881 --> 00:40:29.289
There are beautiful hoods here.

00:40:29.356 --> 00:40:31.133
-Right? And beautiful architecture.
-Yeah.

00:40:31.200 --> 00:40:33.163
And, like,
I have that in another video

00:40:33.230 --> 00:40:35.803
'cause you could think
it's all like this, and it's not.

00:40:36.291 --> 00:40:39.687
This is Stax Recording Studio,
the birthplace of soul music.

00:40:39.754 --> 00:40:41.972
This is why this area
is called Soulsville.

00:40:42.039 --> 00:40:44.789
You can even see
the little Soulsville sign right there.

00:40:44.856 --> 00:40:47.356
-This is a tourist stop?
-Oh, yeah. Big time.

00:40:47.423 --> 00:40:50.421
Okay, so a little piece
of brightness in the hood.

00:40:50.488 --> 00:40:52.733
Well, that was
the backdrop to three murders

00:40:52.800 --> 00:40:55.113
in less than seven days
about a month ago.

00:40:55.587 --> 00:40:57.453
It looks like it should be nice.

00:40:57.520 --> 00:41:01.184
And I'll sit out here
and b*tch about the problems all day long,

00:41:01.251 --> 00:41:03.632
but I don't claim to have the answer.

00:41:03.699 --> 00:41:05.236
There's a lot of answers.

00:41:05.303 --> 00:41:08.636
Getting men to be fathers out here
would go so far.

00:41:09.035 --> 00:41:11.263
You have dudes getting raised.

00:41:11.330 --> 00:41:14.674
Often by aunts or grandmothers,
not even their mother.

00:41:14.741 --> 00:41:16.557
Men need to have men in their lives.

00:41:16.624 --> 00:41:19.469
You need a strong male
in your life to teach you things.

00:41:19.536 --> 00:41:21.749
-Or the gang's gonna do it.
-Or the gang's gonna do it.

00:41:21.816 --> 00:41:23.874
And if you pay attention to social media,

00:41:23.941 --> 00:41:27.221
the things that are important
to a lot of these guys out here,

00:41:27.288 --> 00:41:29.657
like, you know, designer clothes.

00:41:29.724 --> 00:41:32.244
Like sh*t men shouldn't care about, right?

00:41:32.311 --> 00:41:36.184
That is a very strange thing for a man
to be posting on Facebook about.

00:41:36.251 --> 00:41:38.625
But you'll see more often than not,

00:41:38.692 --> 00:41:42.343
that is the content that is
getting echoed out here.

00:41:42.410 --> 00:41:44.799
We need men focused on
building enterprise,

00:41:44.866 --> 00:41:48.337
building businesses,
creating economic opportunity and jobs.

00:41:48.745 --> 00:41:51.393
And if you pay attention to rap lyrics.

00:41:51.460 --> 00:41:53.960
Obviously, we're in
a very heavily Black community.

00:41:54.027 --> 00:41:56.077
I was the only cracker out here for years.

00:41:56.144 --> 00:42:00.593
It's becoming cool again for the Black
entrepreneur and the Black father.

00:42:00.660 --> 00:42:02.466
-It is. The lyrics are coming around.

00:42:02.533 --> 00:42:07.311
I think the timing for what we're trying
to convey out here is God ordained.

00:42:07.378 --> 00:42:09.093
I think we got really lucky.

00:42:09.160 --> 00:42:12.212
You'll see if we get to encounter
any of the dope boys today.

00:42:12.279 --> 00:42:14.892
Like, they want it
to be better out here, too.

00:42:14.959 --> 00:42:16.745
Their kids got to grow up out here.

00:42:16.812 --> 00:42:19.985
Their baby mamas,
their moms are out here on these streets.

00:42:20.052 --> 00:42:23.415
Nobody wants it to
remain what it is now.

00:42:23.482 --> 00:42:26.213
Nobody really just knows how
to go about making change.

00:42:26.280 --> 00:42:29.005
And I think one of the things that
we've gotten good at

00:42:29.078 --> 00:42:33.892
is showing people that
this is so easy a crackhead can do it.

00:42:33.959 --> 00:42:38.910
Just put some effort in and try
to make one thing better at a time.

00:42:38.977 --> 00:42:42.370
And what will end up happening is
what you saw in Wilbur to Melrose.

00:42:42.437 --> 00:42:44.637
Other people are gonna
come along behind you.

00:42:44.704 --> 00:42:46.806
They just need somebody
to lead the charge.

00:42:46.873 --> 00:42:50.323
I think it was Mark Twain that said
in the early days of a revolution,

00:42:50.390 --> 00:42:54.874
a patriot is a hard to find,
something to that effect.

00:42:54.941 --> 00:42:58.240
But when the risk of
being the leader in that goes away,

00:42:58.307 --> 00:43:00.885
the more timid guys
will come out of the woodwork.

00:43:00.952 --> 00:43:02.784
You have to have the more timid guy.

00:43:02.851 --> 00:43:06.939
You have to have everybody coming
together trying to drive change out here.

00:43:07.006 --> 00:43:09.255
It's not something one man can do.

00:43:09.322 --> 00:43:13.241
-Okay, so you grow up in a house,
your parents are split up,

00:43:13.308 --> 00:43:15.912
your mom's on dope, your dad's not there.

00:43:16.633 --> 00:43:20.352
I mean, you don't have
many positive examples.

00:43:20.765 --> 00:43:24.137
-That's the problem, right?
-That's a big part of the problem, yeah.

00:43:24.204 --> 00:43:26.127
And also, mentality,

00:43:26.194 --> 00:43:28.703
easy for me to say
because I don't live here,

00:43:28.770 --> 00:43:31.874
but I was in Savannah recently
talking to this guy.

00:43:31.941 --> 00:43:34.908
It was off camera and he's like,
how do you do it, man?

00:43:34.975 --> 00:43:35.972
And I'm like, what?

00:43:36.039 --> 00:43:39.239
He's like, you travel like this
and you don't cheat on your wife.

00:43:39.306 --> 00:43:41.513
I'm like, I don't know.
I love her and she's great.

00:43:41.580 --> 00:43:42.648
That's not an option?

00:43:42.715 --> 00:43:46.015
He looked at me like I hiked Everest
without a shirt and no oxygen.

00:43:46.082 --> 00:43:47.782
It was the most amazing feat ever.

00:43:47.849 --> 00:43:49.444
And I'm like, man,

00:43:50.120 --> 00:43:55.314
you just blew up your family again
and you keep cheating on women.

00:43:55.381 --> 00:43:56.903
That's just not, I mean…

00:43:56.970 --> 00:43:58.619
Mindset's a huge problem.

00:43:58.686 --> 00:44:01.773
And I'll get the same reaction
from guys out here

00:44:01.840 --> 00:44:04.897
when you sit down and have a
heart-to-heart with a gang member

00:44:04.964 --> 00:44:08.706
or a dope boy and you drop on them
that you don't have to go to prison.

00:44:09.456 --> 00:44:11.042
Nobody has to go to prison.

00:44:11.109 --> 00:44:13.459
-They think they're going no matter what?
-Yeah.

00:44:13.526 --> 00:44:16.332
It's like, this is a part of life
and that gets accepted.

00:44:16.399 --> 00:44:18.830
Infidelity, violent crime,
all of these things

00:44:18.897 --> 00:44:21.576
that should seem so foreign to most of us,

00:44:22.216 --> 00:44:24.679
it is an accepted part of life out here.

00:44:24.746 --> 00:44:29.901
It's so normal that for them
to grasp anything other than that

00:44:29.968 --> 00:44:32.973
is like a shocking, massive revelation.

00:44:33.040 --> 00:44:35.707
But it's a very important revelation
to get out there.

00:44:35.774 --> 00:44:39.241
If you can start getting it to be
generationally accepted,

00:44:39.308 --> 00:44:40.859
you should not be incarcerated.

00:44:40.926 --> 00:44:43.154
That is not… it doesn't have to happen.

00:44:43.720 --> 00:44:45.540
You know, that's kind of a big deal.

00:44:45.607 --> 00:44:49.391
And the sanctity of marriage,
the importance of a home,

00:44:49.826 --> 00:44:54.120
family dinners, things that most of us
probably take for granted, they're,

00:44:54.731 --> 00:44:57.259
you know, that's like something
that happens on TV.

00:44:57.326 --> 00:44:58.444
That's kinda sad.

00:45:02.718 --> 00:45:06.049
Do you see on a governmental level,
what's the strategy?

00:45:06.934 --> 00:45:09.919
You would know better than me since
you're in the thick of it here

00:45:09.986 --> 00:45:14.562
What do you think the strategy is?
Let it be, let it destroy itself or…

00:45:14.629 --> 00:45:18.429
I think both political parties
leverage the hood

00:45:19.358 --> 00:45:21.661
as a con for self interest

00:45:21.728 --> 00:45:26.642
I'm seeing Black America at large
get played by both parties.

00:45:26.709 --> 00:45:29.331
This is not a Democrat or Republican issue.

00:45:29.398 --> 00:45:31.926
They're playing the Black community.

00:45:31.993 --> 00:45:36.621
They're playing on poverty, violent crime
to further their own self interest

00:45:36.688 --> 00:45:38.452
and honestly to get reelected.

00:45:39.026 --> 00:45:43.609
My faith in the institution of
American government has fallen so much

00:45:44.482 --> 00:45:47.243
in the last several years
because of the work we do

00:45:47.310 --> 00:45:52.097
I don't see government
as actually really wanting to solve thing.

00:45:52.164 --> 00:45:54.864
I don't see the politicians
that are out here clammering

00:45:54.931 --> 00:45:57.032
for everybody's vote and claiming

00:45:57.099 --> 00:46:00.791
to have done so much
for the hood actually do sh*t.

00:46:00.858 --> 00:46:04.011
I don't. For the most part,
especially at the federal level,

00:46:04.078 --> 00:46:07.593
our government uses the hood
as a means to an end.

00:46:07.660 --> 00:46:10.463
They use Black America
as a means to an end.

00:46:10.530 --> 00:46:12.499
-'Cause if you get that vote you win.

00:46:12.566 --> 00:46:14.433
-Absolutely.
-Or it's gonna help.

00:46:14.500 --> 00:46:18.782
Or you get money
legislated away for self interest.

00:46:18.849 --> 00:46:23.551
You look at the fraud that's going on
all around our country, federal programs,

00:46:23.618 --> 00:46:26.932
and so many of them hinge on your ability

00:46:26.999 --> 00:46:29.753
to sell that idea to the Black community.

00:46:30.490 --> 00:46:35.045
I have been waiting for
the successful Black community to show up

00:46:35.112 --> 00:46:38.861
and shoulder this load with us and it's
not happened and that's frustrating.

00:46:38.928 --> 00:46:42.422
You've got a lot of very successful
Black businessmen in this city

00:46:42.489 --> 00:46:45.081
and all around the country
who came out of Memphis

00:46:45.148 --> 00:46:49.065
and we're out here trying to make
the worst hood in America

00:46:49.132 --> 00:46:50.895
a safer place for kids.

00:46:50.962 --> 00:46:52.814
And we've got metrics to back it up,

00:46:52.888 --> 00:46:55.202
it's not just me saying
we're making it better.

00:46:55.269 --> 00:46:57.390
We're objectively making this place better.

00:46:57.457 --> 00:46:59.543
The crime has dropped significantly.

00:46:59.610 --> 00:47:03.303
You've got construction happening
on streets in South Memphis

00:47:03.370 --> 00:47:05.385
that hasn't happened in a hundred years.

00:47:05.452 --> 00:47:09.573
Right? And there's no way to say it's not
because of what we've done out here.

00:47:09.640 --> 00:47:13.146
I feel especially
our elected officials from that community

00:47:13.213 --> 00:47:14.973
should be showing up out here.

00:47:15.040 --> 00:47:16.633
There's some great art in here.

00:47:16.700 --> 00:47:19.632
-This is beautiful.
-That's why I wanted to bring you here.

00:47:19.699 --> 00:47:21.045
This is really cool.

00:47:21.118 --> 00:47:24.435
There is so much
hidden potential in the hood.

00:47:24.502 --> 00:47:26.884
-Right look at that.
-People just don't realize it.

00:47:26.951 --> 00:47:28.084
-No, it's so true.

00:47:28.151 --> 00:47:31.553
Every time you come out here
the art's different.

00:47:31.932 --> 00:47:35.140
This is still active,
there's still kids skating out here.

00:47:35.207 --> 00:47:36.125
-Look at that.

00:47:43.969 --> 00:47:46.384
[Ben] We're getting into Midtown now.

00:47:46.451 --> 00:47:49.039
You'll have million dollar homes
in a second.

00:47:49.541 --> 00:47:52.604
Matter of fact,
I believe Scientology owns this one.

00:47:52.671 --> 00:47:53.659
-No way.
-Yeah.

00:47:54.538 --> 00:47:58.758
-That is a trip.
I mean that's literally 30 seconds.

00:47:58.825 --> 00:47:59.822
-30 seconds, man.

00:48:00.240 --> 00:48:02.146
-Does the crime come over here?

00:48:02.213 --> 00:48:04.113
-100% yeah.
-100%?

00:48:04.180 --> 00:48:06.530
Usually the crime's
gonna happen on the streets.

00:48:06.597 --> 00:48:11.146
Home invasions are exceedingly rare
when you're talking about normal citizens.

00:48:11.213 --> 00:48:14.763
If you're selling dope, your house
is getting broken into I promise.

00:48:14.830 --> 00:48:18.047
People pretty much just accept it
but that being said,

00:48:18.114 --> 00:48:22.362
you just walked through the ultra hood
and saw nobody's bothering you.

00:48:22.830 --> 00:48:25.687
As long as you act like
you're where you're supposed to be

00:48:25.754 --> 00:48:27.685
you're not gonna become a victim.

00:48:27.752 --> 00:48:29.710
Most are crimes of opportunity.

00:48:29.777 --> 00:48:34.238
If they can target somebody that looks like
a victim they're 100% gonna do that.

00:48:38.000 --> 00:48:40.776
-[Jess] This is Central Barbecue.
-This is her favorite restaurant.

00:48:40.843 --> 00:48:43.621
I love barbecue period
but Central is so good.

00:48:43.688 --> 00:48:46.963
-And Memphis obviously legendary.
-Legendary barbecue, yeah.

00:48:47.749 --> 00:48:51.646
It's better here than anywhere else
and I will fight people over that.

00:48:53.531 --> 00:48:55.273
-Thank you so much.
-Yes, sir.

00:48:55.340 --> 00:48:58.694
-Do you love it? Is it amazing.
-Oh, it's amazing.

00:48:58.761 --> 00:48:59.902
Okay.

00:48:59.969 --> 00:49:00.852
Okay, cool.

00:49:05.780 --> 00:49:07.604
-[Peter] Thank you.
-No problem.

00:49:09.659 --> 00:49:11.542
All right, pulled pork sandwich.

00:49:12.495 --> 00:49:14.435
-What is that, Ben?
-Barbecue nachos.

00:49:14.502 --> 00:49:15.739
Barbecue nachos.

00:49:16.292 --> 00:49:19.623
-That's a little bit of everything, huh?
-Little bit of everything.

00:49:19.690 --> 00:49:24.697
In the next couple years we need to have
about $10 million raised

00:49:25.842 --> 00:49:27.649
to open our own treatment center.

00:49:27.716 --> 00:49:30.231
-We already have the building.
-Okay.

00:49:30.298 --> 00:49:33.772
Once we have that done
it'll be off to the races

00:49:33.839 --> 00:49:35.663
That'll be generating revenue.

00:49:36.514 --> 00:49:39.664
-How's it generating revenue.
-Treatment center. Insurance.

00:49:39.731 --> 00:49:40.951
-Okay.
-Yeah.

00:49:41.018 --> 00:49:42.800
And state reimbursement.

00:49:43.726 --> 00:49:48.599
So why do you think that link of the chain
is broken with treatment centers?

00:49:48.990 --> 00:49:50.237
Just bad stigma?

00:49:50.304 --> 00:49:52.537
Or what is it? 'Cause it seems like…

00:49:52.604 --> 00:49:55.629
-I don't know that it's broken.
-We need more of that, right?

00:49:55.696 --> 00:50:01.136
People that are in that space in Memphis
and probably nationwide are very

00:50:02.852 --> 00:50:03.749
guarded.

00:50:03.816 --> 00:50:06.981
They view every other
center as competition.

00:50:07.048 --> 00:50:09.107
There's not a lot of collaboration.

00:50:09.962 --> 00:50:14.310
And it's easy for us not to look at anybody
as competition 'cause this mission

00:50:14.813 --> 00:50:16.242
is not my livelihood.

00:50:16.309 --> 00:50:18.432
My income doesn't depend on this.

00:50:19.357 --> 00:50:21.200
In fact it actually hurts my income.

00:50:21.267 --> 00:50:25.875
Right and the more I get into the stories
around the country, the more I'm wary

00:50:26.820 --> 00:50:29.859
when an income is
fully off something like this.

00:50:29.926 --> 00:50:30.844
It's sketchy.

00:50:30.911 --> 00:50:32.909
Especially if they're driving
a truck like yours.

00:50:32.976 --> 00:50:35.473
-Ah, what's this dude doing with the money?
-Bingo.

00:50:35.540 --> 00:50:38.250
And I hate to say it,
it's more often than not

00:50:38.317 --> 00:50:40.244
where I see situations where it's like

00:50:40.311 --> 00:50:45.321
I love the cause, the mission, and then
greed comes when money comes in.

00:50:46.140 --> 00:50:48.571
-Well…
-You make no money from…

00:50:48.638 --> 00:50:51.270
This costs me
six figures personally every year.

00:50:51.337 --> 00:50:53.867
-You have a well performing business?
-Yeah.

00:50:54.873 --> 00:50:55.756
…supports you.

00:50:56.226 --> 00:50:59.635
Despite talking about that often and loudly

00:51:00.121 --> 00:51:03.235
people still see me
and my collection of muscle cars

00:51:03.302 --> 00:51:05.678
and my truck and somehow
get it in their head that

00:51:05.745 --> 00:51:08.421
that the non-profit
has paid for any of that.

00:51:08.488 --> 00:51:12.386
I'm like, no, I had all of these things
before I had a non-profit.

00:51:12.453 --> 00:51:13.336
Right.

00:51:13.403 --> 00:51:16.913
The thing that frustrates me the most is
non-profit financials are public.

00:51:16.980 --> 00:51:19.332
You don't even have to
do the research yourself.

00:51:19.399 --> 00:51:22.995
You can ask Chat GPT how much
does Ben make from We Fight Monsters?

00:51:23.062 --> 00:51:25.176
It'll tell you straight up, zero dollars.

00:51:25.243 --> 00:51:27.562
-'Cause it can see our financials.
-Okay.

00:51:28.760 --> 00:51:30.440
Great sandwich by the way.

00:51:30.906 --> 00:51:32.388
Really good barbecue.

00:51:34.258 --> 00:51:35.141
Mmm.

00:51:35.706 --> 00:51:37.188
I just love barbecue.

00:51:38.274 --> 00:51:39.157
Tender.

00:51:40.363 --> 00:51:43.263
Okay, so what's the five year plan
if you have one?

00:51:43.687 --> 00:51:46.731
We'll have 777,
our treatment center opened up.

00:51:47.635 --> 00:51:50.649
We'll have a trade school,
job skills training.

00:51:51.946 --> 00:51:54.440
A full reentry program, youth services.

00:51:55.300 --> 00:51:57.940
And I won't have to be
the face of it anymore.

00:51:58.354 --> 00:52:00.060
-You don't want to be?
-Hm-mm.

00:52:00.796 --> 00:52:03.502
I want to focus on my businesses
and my family.

00:52:03.871 --> 00:52:06.864
You don't want to work on
anything else in this space?

00:52:09.198 --> 00:52:12.331
I want to tell myself I don't
but I know that's a lie.

00:52:14.266 --> 00:52:16.888
I'm incapable of not doing this.

00:52:17.656 --> 00:52:19.833
I want to expand our work of reentry.

00:52:21.549 --> 00:52:24.667
I want to have more done
with youth programming.

00:52:25.101 --> 00:52:26.984
-Okay.
-That's a difficult space…

00:52:27.981 --> 00:52:30.216
…to be effective and that's a big gap.

00:52:30.804 --> 00:52:33.417
Just you're dealing with state, parents?

00:52:33.491 --> 00:52:34.374
Liability.

00:52:35.129 --> 00:52:36.174
Legality.

00:52:36.241 --> 00:52:37.511
Public perception.

00:52:38.574 --> 00:52:42.502
Recovery period is
a tough space to operate in.

00:52:43.070 --> 00:52:45.129
Because there are so many grifters.

00:52:45.542 --> 00:52:46.425
Right.

00:52:46.492 --> 00:52:49.627
There are so many schemes and scams
and it's impossible

00:52:51.225 --> 00:52:53.148
to not get lumped in with those people.

00:52:53.215 --> 00:52:56.674
Doesn't matter how successful
I am on the for profit side.

00:52:56.741 --> 00:52:58.741
-Yeah.
-People are always gonna see

00:52:59.587 --> 00:53:03.560
Ben just made a post asking for money
for this but Ben has a…

00:53:04.071 --> 00:53:06.653
A $90,000 truck and a 1970 GTX.

00:53:07.328 --> 00:53:10.863
Okay, well Ben's already given
a lot of money to this.

00:53:11.596 --> 00:53:15.583
What are you doing for your business?
What's your main business?

00:53:16.757 --> 00:53:19.757
It's actually shifting, ones taking off.

00:53:20.239 --> 00:53:24.219
For years my primary business
has been a company called Black Rifle.

00:53:24.286 --> 00:53:27.729
We do data intelligence and digital media
for the firearms industry.

00:53:27.796 --> 00:53:30.510
Gun companies can't go advertise in Google.

00:53:30.577 --> 00:53:32.765
-They can't advertise on Facebook.
-Okay.

00:53:32.832 --> 00:53:36.343
We created a platform that allows
them to advertise across ad exchanges

00:53:36.410 --> 00:53:40.832
like they were with Google and Facebook
and it's been very successful.

00:53:41.417 --> 00:53:44.354
Then we started
another company called Once American

00:53:44.772 --> 00:53:48.612
that does open source intelligence
targeting in investigative work.

00:53:49.140 --> 00:53:52.872
Basically we leverage agentic AI to

00:53:53.793 --> 00:53:57.549
scour petabytes of information.

00:53:58.595 --> 00:54:01.311
to do intelligence work on bad actors.

00:54:01.863 --> 00:54:05.423
And it can do in two minutes
what it would take a human a day or two.

00:54:05.490 --> 00:54:08.366
Give me an example how that works.
There's a bad actor…

00:54:08.433 --> 00:54:11.601
Let's say Homeland Security has
a trafficking case

00:54:11.668 --> 00:54:13.780
tied back to an email address.

00:54:13.847 --> 00:54:17.851
I can take that email, run it through
and give you that guy's full identity

00:54:17.918 --> 00:54:20.407
I'm talking down to
the VIN number of his vehicle.

00:54:20.474 --> 00:54:23.644
And they're able to go
action that as law enforcement.

00:54:24.182 --> 00:54:25.065
Gotcha.

00:54:25.132 --> 00:54:29.563
And so that's actually starting to
take off really well right now.

00:54:29.630 --> 00:54:30.681
I bet. I bet.

00:54:37.475 --> 00:54:41.525
[Peter] We're going to North Memphis
which is a totally different hood, right?

00:54:41.592 --> 00:54:44.083
-Totally different hood,
very little crossover.

00:54:44.150 --> 00:54:45.979
You hear me talk about street gangs,

00:54:46.046 --> 00:54:48.878
They're very lowly organized in Memphis.

00:54:48.945 --> 00:54:52.711
The fact they get hit with RICO charges,
racketeering and organized crime

00:54:52.778 --> 00:54:54.083
is hilarious to me.

00:54:54.150 --> 00:54:56.319
Street gangs in Memphis are not like that.

00:54:56.386 --> 00:55:00.455
It's not like there's blood in, blood out,
they're just cliques, man.

00:55:00.977 --> 00:55:04.258
And for the most part
a lot of the violence is intra-clique.

00:55:04.325 --> 00:55:06.500
It's they're fighting each other.

00:55:06.567 --> 00:55:11.576
-Okay. So North Memphis, rough,
just like South Memphis or different?

00:55:11.880 --> 00:55:13.409
 It depends on who you ask.

00:55:13.947 --> 00:55:17.134
Statistically, it's nowhere near as bad
as South Memphis.

00:55:17.201 --> 00:55:19.701
But, you know,
you hit some of these neighborhoods,

00:55:19.768 --> 00:55:23.099
it's gonna feel a lot worse,
if that makes sense.

00:55:23.166 --> 00:55:24.683
It looks worse.

00:55:24.750 --> 00:55:26.999
This is like the weirdest little
compound area

00:55:27.066 --> 00:55:29.230
and it is full of addicts.

00:55:29.297 --> 00:55:32.833
They closed it down because of black mold.
Now it's opened again.

00:55:32.900 --> 00:55:33.959
But you know what?

00:55:34.026 --> 00:55:37.580
There are a lot of just
good working class people in here.

00:55:37.647 --> 00:55:40.203
But addiction is
a big problem out here, man.

00:55:40.270 --> 00:55:43.264
These people had started squatting
in these when they were shut down

00:55:43.331 --> 00:55:45.588
and they built barricades out of pallets.

00:55:45.655 --> 00:55:48.035
It was like some
real meth lab looking sh*t.

00:55:48.102 --> 00:55:50.183
In fact, they were probably cooking meth.

00:55:50.595 --> 00:55:52.345
Some of them don't even have power.

00:55:52.412 --> 00:55:55.826
It looks a lot better now because
they slapped paint and new roof on.

00:55:55.893 --> 00:55:58.893
But dude, these things,
like they're rough on the inside.

00:56:03.311 --> 00:56:05.726
So guy just recycling stuff.
Is that that story?

00:56:05.800 --> 00:56:07.315
Never know, man.

00:56:08.495 --> 00:56:09.966
That could be his family.

00:56:10.564 --> 00:56:14.114
Now sometimes they'll be scrapping sh*t.
There are a lot of scrap yards.

00:56:14.181 --> 00:56:16.881
I didn't see what he had.
They'll pick up scrap metal.

00:56:16.948 --> 00:56:21.239
But the scrap yards out here are allies
when you're trying to get intel

00:56:21.306 --> 00:56:24.867
on trafficking survivors
who might be scrapping sh*t.

00:56:25.942 --> 00:56:29.063
We had one guy gonna look out
for a pedophile for us.

00:56:29.130 --> 00:56:32.758
-Okay, so what's the trafficking scene
these days with women?

00:56:33.338 --> 00:56:37.463
So trafficking in Memphis
is largely dope-fueled prostitution.

00:56:37.530 --> 00:56:42.001
We've sat down with human traffickers,
convicted traffickers, and asked them,

00:56:42.551 --> 00:56:46.895
if you were gonna start that back up,
how would you go through that process?

00:56:46.962 --> 00:56:51.042
And none of them said they're gonna kidnap
girls and get them addicted to dope.

00:56:51.109 --> 00:56:52.487
Because they don't have to.

00:56:52.554 --> 00:56:55.111
There are plenty of people
who are already addicted.

00:56:55.178 --> 00:56:58.277
There are plenty of girls
who already have problems at home.

00:56:58.344 --> 00:57:00.107
They're gonna be easily exploitable

00:57:00.174 --> 00:57:02.344
because they have trust issues,
unmet needs.

00:57:02.411 --> 00:57:05.324
They have basic needs unmet.
Food, clothing, housing.

00:57:05.391 --> 00:57:06.942
You go meet any of those needs.

00:57:07.009 --> 00:57:09.895
And before long
you're gonna have essentially a slave.

00:57:09.962 --> 00:57:12.288
-Especially if they're already addicted.
-Okay.

00:57:12.355 --> 00:57:14.824
And so trafficking
more often than not begins

00:57:15.237 --> 00:57:19.521
with a girl who has already
maybe used her body to fund her addiction.

00:57:19.588 --> 00:57:23.538
And just falls in with the wrong dude
who acts like he's gonna take care of her.

00:57:23.605 --> 00:57:27.119
And what ends up happening is
he's now got him a new victim to traffic.

00:57:27.186 --> 00:57:31.253
As long as you keep her fed with dope,
she's gonna keep doing what's asked.

00:57:31.320 --> 00:57:33.553
So just pimp-ho relationship basically?

00:57:33.620 --> 00:57:34.543
-100%. Yep.

00:57:34.610 --> 00:57:37.634
-Okay. How many workers
do these pimps have typically?

00:57:37.701 --> 00:57:41.233
Most of them will have,
you know, two to three main ones.

00:57:41.300 --> 00:57:44.456
And then they'll have those
referred to as the bottoms.

00:57:44.523 --> 00:57:47.973
Some of them have just one primary bottom
and they'll go recruit more.

00:57:48.040 --> 00:57:51.540
I think the most we've ever tied back
to one trafficker is what, seven?

00:57:51.607 --> 00:57:52.440
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:57:52.507 --> 00:57:54.475
They're not big rings here.

00:57:54.542 --> 00:57:56.342
Jess will end up working these cases

00:57:56.409 --> 00:57:59.793
because a lot of them
start on sex-ad sites.

00:57:59.860 --> 00:58:02.058
And I'm just not gonna sit there and…

00:58:02.125 --> 00:58:06.325
But they'll tweak out on these things
and start identifying girls based on ads.

00:58:06.392 --> 00:58:08.006
I'm seeing signs of trafficking.

00:58:08.073 --> 00:58:10.873
I've tied this number back
to this guy using the platform

00:58:10.940 --> 00:58:12.498
that Homeland Security uses.

00:58:12.565 --> 00:58:14.525
You'll tie that back to the trafficker.

00:58:14.592 --> 00:58:18.762
We've identified rings that'll operate
at Airbnbs in Houston, New Orleans,

00:58:19.105 --> 00:58:20.688
Memphis, Miami, Tampa.

00:58:21.094 --> 00:58:22.668
And they'll own these Airbnbs.

00:58:22.735 --> 00:58:27.564
Like, these are big deal trafficking rings
that are run by dudes and their bottoms.

00:58:27.631 --> 00:58:30.199
And so those end up getting
kind of large sometimes.

00:58:30.272 --> 00:58:32.673
And those are the kind of cases
we'll work with,

00:58:32.740 --> 00:58:34.622
you know, three-letter agencies.

00:58:35.134 --> 00:58:37.034
-Where is it the worst in the country?

00:58:37.101 --> 00:58:41.233
It's pretty much uniform
across urban, metro area America.

00:58:41.300 --> 00:58:43.030
You're seeing this everywhere.

00:58:43.097 --> 00:58:47.835
You see it everywhere. I think, and we've
worked counter-trafficking operations

00:58:47.902 --> 00:58:50.486
and recovered women all over the country.

00:58:50.553 --> 00:58:52.809
I think where it's gonna be the worst

00:58:53.296 --> 00:58:56.017
is where you have
a larger immigrant population

00:58:56.084 --> 00:59:00.047
and you've got people that
you can really exploit

00:59:00.114 --> 00:59:03.238
in a number of different ways.
Not just based around addiction.

00:59:03.305 --> 00:59:07.932
But like if you're running an operation
that involves a passport scam.

00:59:07.999 --> 00:59:10.999
Like a Chinese illicit massage parlor
or something like that.

00:59:11.066 --> 00:59:13.232
Those are where
it gets really, really ugly.

00:59:13.299 --> 00:59:17.447
And so cities, like Memphis does not have
a very large immigrant population

00:59:17.514 --> 00:59:19.792
compared to Atlanta, Houston, DFW.

00:59:19.859 --> 00:59:20.791
Okay.

00:59:20.858 --> 00:59:24.829
Memphis and St. Louis are a lot alike
in our demographics and our issues.

00:59:25.279 --> 00:59:28.213
Atlanta and Houston are a lot alike
in their issues.

00:59:28.635 --> 00:59:33.705
And so the more nefarious side
of human trafficking

00:59:33.772 --> 00:59:35.243
turns up in those cities.

00:59:35.713 --> 00:59:39.160
Here, most of it is based around addiction.

00:59:42.830 --> 00:59:45.646
So this is an area
known as Speedway Terrace.

00:59:45.713 --> 00:59:49.348
Not a great part of town,
but it's one of my favorite places.

00:59:50.200 --> 00:59:52.512
Now all of these homes
are about a century old.

00:59:52.579 --> 00:59:54.136
-What are these going for?

00:59:54.203 --> 00:59:56.568
-It depends on how they've been renovated.

00:59:56.635 --> 00:59:59.621
One right down there
got renovated really nice, like Airbnb.

00:59:59.688 --> 01:00:02.276
And they were selling it for,
what, $270,000?

01:00:02.343 --> 01:00:05.963
-So in Memphis you might get an Airbnb
and not know what hood you're in?

01:00:06.030 --> 01:00:06.823
Right.

01:00:07.232 --> 01:00:10.409
-And you could end up in the hood.
-Absolutely. [laughs]

01:00:10.950 --> 01:00:14.011
We call this our old men's home
or the retirement home.

01:00:14.078 --> 01:00:15.343
And it's not intentional.

01:00:15.409 --> 01:00:17.909
It's just usually where
all of our old vets end up.

01:00:17.976 --> 01:00:21.435
Because our other men's house
is reserved for drug court clients.

01:00:21.873 --> 01:00:25.405
Which not always,
but usually ends up being younger guys.

01:00:28.420 --> 01:00:30.320
Two of my favorite guys on earth, man.

01:00:30.387 --> 01:00:31.328
How y'all doing?

01:00:31.881 --> 01:00:34.638
-How's it going, bro? Love you, dude.
-Good to see you.

01:00:35.447 --> 01:00:37.802
-Hey, buddy. How's it going?
-I'm all right.

01:00:37.869 --> 01:00:39.818
-How you doing? Peter.
-How you doing?

01:00:39.885 --> 01:00:42.642
-Nice to meet you,
-Gary, Peter, Peter, Gary.

01:00:42.709 --> 01:00:43.591
This is Drenon.

01:00:44.706 --> 01:00:47.082
-Drenon. Nice to meet you, sir.
-How you doing?

01:00:47.149 --> 01:00:48.013
Doing well.

01:00:48.080 --> 01:00:50.614
He met your sister earlier
and your granddaughter.

01:00:50.681 --> 01:00:52.823
Okay. Rekaya. The little girl.

01:00:52.890 --> 01:00:54.432
-That's his granddaughter.
-Nice.

01:00:54.499 --> 01:00:55.623
She's doing good, man.

01:00:55.690 --> 01:00:58.490
-How are things going right now for you?
-Hey, fine, man.

01:00:59.212 --> 01:01:01.057
Going pretty good for me, man.

01:01:01.124 --> 01:01:03.379
-Sober life.
-How long you been sober?

01:01:03.970 --> 01:01:05.235
Well, '23.

01:01:06.272 --> 01:01:08.093
Since '23? Oh, nice.

01:01:08.160 --> 01:01:10.213
-And you, sir?
-13 months.

01:01:10.280 --> 01:01:14.220
-This time.
-Okay, this time, how many relapses?

01:01:15.470 --> 01:01:16.669
At least six.

01:01:16.736 --> 01:01:17.822
-Oh, wow.

01:01:18.542 --> 01:01:20.466
So how you holding on this time?

01:01:20.533 --> 01:01:21.974
This time I get more active.

01:01:22.041 --> 01:01:25.063
Ben gave me the opportunity
to be the manager of the house.

01:01:25.130 --> 01:01:26.130
That helps a lot.

01:01:27.910 --> 01:01:29.337
And then I'll tell you what,

01:01:29.404 --> 01:01:34.062
what really made me
take help this time was

01:01:34.129 --> 01:01:37.056
When I come back in here,
I come back in with my head down.

01:01:37.123 --> 01:01:39.072
I done messed up again and again.

01:01:39.139 --> 01:01:41.339
And that ain't something
that I choose to do.

01:01:41.406 --> 01:01:44.109
That's something that just happens
for whatever reason.

01:01:44.176 --> 01:01:48.420
But when I come in here and Ben and Cody
and them, they never look down on me.

01:01:48.487 --> 01:01:52.070
He just say, hey, you got it this time.
I'm glad to see you back.

01:01:52.137 --> 01:01:54.443
And that means more than anything.

01:01:54.860 --> 01:01:56.915
And it pushes me forward.

01:01:57.355 --> 01:01:59.675
I want to do that for somebody else.
You know?

01:01:59.742 --> 01:02:03.202
-And he's done it for a lot
of somebody else's at this point.

01:02:03.269 --> 01:02:04.102
A lot!

01:02:04.482 --> 01:02:07.080
When they give me
the opportunity to do it,

01:02:07.147 --> 01:02:10.088
you don't know how much it means
for somebody that…

01:02:10.155 --> 01:02:12.449
I know I've relapsed six times.

01:02:12.516 --> 01:02:16.079
I know I've accumulated
more charges in the last five years

01:02:16.146 --> 01:02:17.736
than I had in my whole life.

01:02:17.803 --> 01:02:21.413
But for him not to look down on me,
for him to say, you got it this time.

01:02:21.487 --> 01:02:23.437
For whatever reason, it just took hold.

01:02:23.504 --> 01:02:26.775
And then me being given
the opportunity to be proactive,

01:02:26.842 --> 01:02:29.013
to volunteer and do other things.

01:02:29.080 --> 01:02:32.756
And I don't want to let him down again.
I don't want to let me down again.

01:02:32.823 --> 01:02:34.378
-You never let me down, man.

01:02:34.453 --> 01:02:36.053
And he showed me that.

01:02:36.120 --> 01:02:38.334
-Can we walk in here? Is that cool?

01:02:38.940 --> 01:02:41.159
How many of you guys in here right now?

01:02:41.226 --> 01:02:42.798
-I think it's six.
-Six?

01:02:46.839 --> 01:02:49.856
For six guys, you keep a…

01:02:49.923 --> 01:02:51.206
How you doing, sir?

01:02:51.273 --> 01:02:53.598
For six guys, you keep a pretty tight ship.

01:02:53.665 --> 01:02:54.548
Three, four…

01:02:55.780 --> 01:02:57.155
It holds 12.

01:02:57.236 --> 01:02:58.322
It's low right now.

01:02:58.389 --> 01:03:01.126
-I've never seen a place
with six guys this clean.

01:03:01.193 --> 01:03:03.054
Oh, brother,
they keep this place spotless.

01:03:03.121 --> 01:03:04.862
-Good work.
-How you doing?

01:03:04.929 --> 01:03:05.762
I'm Ben.

01:03:06.393 --> 01:03:07.913
-Nice to meet you.
-Nice to meet you too.

01:03:07.980 --> 01:03:09.274
This is my wife, Jess.

01:03:09.699 --> 01:03:12.025
-Peter.
-Nice to meet y'all.

01:03:12.092 --> 01:03:13.405
-Your name is?
-I'm Gary.

01:03:13.485 --> 01:03:14.484
-Gary?
-Yes, sir.

01:03:14.551 --> 01:03:16.442
[Jess] You got here
not too long ago, right?

01:03:16.509 --> 01:03:17.918
-Two weeks ago.
-Okay.

01:03:18.305 --> 01:03:20.593
-[Peter] How's it going, Gary?
-It's going good.

01:03:20.660 --> 01:03:22.671
-You guys all get along?
-Yes we do.

01:03:22.738 --> 01:03:24.379
Who's not doing the dishes, huh?

01:03:24.446 --> 01:03:26.691
Everybody does the dishes.

01:03:26.758 --> 01:03:27.640
Week at a time.

01:03:29.046 --> 01:03:30.105
Oh, this is great.

01:03:31.510 --> 01:03:34.862
Yeah, so they have their own lockers
and keep their own stuff.

01:03:34.929 --> 01:03:38.110
This is like a community kitchen, so…

01:03:38.177 --> 01:03:40.043
It's all organized. Look at that.

01:03:40.110 --> 01:03:42.307
They got the parts of the fridge.

01:03:43.175 --> 01:03:47.351
Yeah, we had another guy that was
running it a while back, and he was a…

01:03:48.463 --> 01:03:50.776
I'll just say it.
He's a horrible human being.

01:03:50.843 --> 01:03:54.173
So we had to get him out,
and then Gary's in it now,

01:03:54.240 --> 01:03:58.053
and it's always been big
to keep it very tidy and clean.

01:03:58.120 --> 01:03:59.171
-Yeah, it looks great.

01:03:59.238 --> 01:04:01.963
It's more orderly,
and it gives them more structure.

01:04:02.030 --> 01:04:04.530
God, they're doing really good.
It looks beautiful.

01:04:04.597 --> 01:04:07.043
Yeah, for six guys? No dishes in the sink?

01:04:07.110 --> 01:04:08.377
-Come on.
-Yeah.

01:04:08.444 --> 01:04:09.327
That's a first.

01:04:10.210 --> 01:04:13.718
And it makes me feel even better
seeing the men that come through here.

01:04:13.785 --> 01:04:17.434
Some of them we used to smoke crack
with Jordan back in the trap house.

01:04:17.501 --> 01:04:19.713
-And to have him here now, clean.
-No way.

01:04:19.780 --> 01:04:21.591
It's just an amazing feeling.

01:04:22.794 --> 01:04:25.166
You know what I love the most about this?

01:04:25.233 --> 01:04:28.140
It's a feeling and I'm sure
it will come through on camera.

01:04:28.207 --> 01:04:30.567
These people love you guys.
There's a deep bond.

01:04:30.634 --> 01:04:33.683
-Yeah.
-We've been through a lot with these guys.

01:04:33.750 --> 01:04:36.757
Both the ones we knew in
active addiction and the ones since.

01:04:36.824 --> 01:04:39.224
You get to stand by them
through thick and thin.

01:04:39.291 --> 01:04:41.091
When we were in the house at Wilbur,

01:04:41.158 --> 01:04:43.758
I told you a good friend
overdosed and died in there.

01:04:43.825 --> 01:04:45.155
That was his wife.

01:04:45.222 --> 01:04:46.895
Yeah, so like, we go back.

01:04:46.962 --> 01:04:47.844
We go way back.

01:04:49.000 --> 01:04:51.326
-And you got drug testing here.
-Oh, yeah.

01:04:51.393 --> 01:04:53.563
You gotta have accountability, man.

01:04:53.630 --> 01:04:55.193
It's tough when somebody fails.

01:04:55.260 --> 01:04:57.360
The way these houses
were historically run,

01:04:57.427 --> 01:05:00.066
this has been a halfway house
20 years in Memphis.

01:05:00.133 --> 01:05:03.733
I was court-ordered into the one
up the street. We own that one now, too.

01:05:03.800 --> 01:05:06.805
I got kicked out in eight days
'cause I failed a drug screen.

01:05:06.872 --> 01:05:09.072
But that historically,
that's how it was run.

01:05:09.139 --> 01:05:11.438
If you failed, you're out. You know?

01:05:11.960 --> 01:05:15.172
And it's still that way today,
but it's not just you're out.

01:05:15.239 --> 01:05:18.648
It's, alright, you failed this screen.
You're out of here today.

01:05:18.715 --> 01:05:22.252
But I can take you to detox,
and I can save your bed.

01:05:23.000 --> 01:05:24.614
Or you can just hit the streets.

01:05:24.681 --> 01:05:27.179
And so, we've had a lot of guys opt

01:05:27.838 --> 01:05:29.842
to just start over, you know?

01:05:29.909 --> 01:05:32.056
And that's what it takes sometimes.

01:05:32.123 --> 01:05:34.571
For some of us, like me, we're dumb.

01:05:34.638 --> 01:05:38.212
And you gotta hit the streets
and go suffer some more, you know?

01:05:39.350 --> 01:05:41.650
Guys, thanks for letting us
crash this morning.

01:05:41.717 --> 01:05:43.794
-Keep up the great work.
-I love you, man.

01:05:43.861 --> 01:05:45.053
Hey man, thank you.

01:05:46.088 --> 01:05:47.417
Thank you, man. Thank you.

01:05:47.484 --> 01:05:49.345
Thank you. I f*ckin' love you, bro.

01:05:49.926 --> 01:05:52.248
-Gary, thanks, man.
-Take care.

01:05:52.315 --> 01:05:53.433
We'll see you, man.

01:05:54.160 --> 01:05:56.395
-Nice to meet you.
-Take care, brother.

01:06:05.739 --> 01:06:08.989
-So, I haven't seen that much of this.
-You won't.

01:06:09.056 --> 01:06:11.326
The police typically
won't tolerate that sh*t.

01:06:11.393 --> 01:06:13.981
There are some places
where it'll pop up and…

01:06:16.160 --> 01:06:18.572
-He probably coming to yell at you.
-Yeah.

01:06:20.310 --> 01:06:22.837
-It is horrendous.
-What? The public schools?

01:06:22.904 --> 01:06:25.252
The public school system here is just…

01:06:25.319 --> 01:06:29.483
And there's so much drama
around the school system right now.

01:06:29.550 --> 01:06:33.209
They gave up their charter.
They can't run a school system.

01:06:33.276 --> 01:06:34.666
That's the problem.

01:06:34.733 --> 01:06:37.342
Nobody is proficient at grade level.

01:06:37.746 --> 01:06:42.018
I forget the percentage that is actually
even able to read when they graduate.

01:06:42.085 --> 01:06:43.645
But it is abhorrent.

01:06:44.340 --> 01:06:46.858
And to look at that
and then expect any different

01:06:46.925 --> 01:06:49.043
from the next generation of Memphis…

01:06:49.784 --> 01:06:51.338
It feels a little disingenuous.

01:06:51.405 --> 01:06:53.858
Like, you can't throw kids into

01:06:54.447 --> 01:06:59.636
such a hot garbage mess of public school
and expect change to happen.

01:06:59.703 --> 01:07:01.713
If anything, it's gonna get worse.

01:07:01.780 --> 01:07:04.980
You can't blame it all on the schools.
It's the home life, right?

01:07:05.047 --> 01:07:07.653
Absolutely. There's a myriad of factors.

01:07:07.720 --> 01:07:12.350
The talent and the heart is leaving
the teaching profession by and large.

01:07:13.178 --> 01:07:15.944
But also, you've got kids
coming into schools

01:07:16.011 --> 01:07:18.070
acting like grown adults that have…

01:07:18.758 --> 01:07:22.284
They don't attach consequences
to actions at all.

01:07:22.351 --> 01:07:25.403
They don't process
that the teachers have authority.

01:07:25.470 --> 01:07:28.121
Or if they do
it means absolutely nothing to them.

01:07:28.188 --> 01:07:30.655
That's all society's
and the adults' fault, right?

01:07:30.722 --> 01:07:32.843
-For letting it go to that?
-Absolutely.

01:07:32.910 --> 01:07:35.551
Because if I was a kid,
and I did as a kid,

01:07:35.618 --> 01:07:37.363
I pushed it right to the edge.

01:07:37.430 --> 01:07:38.529
100%. I did, too.

01:07:38.596 --> 01:07:41.796
And if the edge was further away,
I would have pushed it further.

01:07:41.863 --> 01:07:44.223
Yep. Absolutely.
That's what adolescents do.

01:07:44.290 --> 01:07:46.843
And that's part of
what makes America great

01:07:46.910 --> 01:07:48.719
is that our kids push limits, right?

01:07:48.786 --> 01:07:51.987
-But you've got to have guardrails up.
-Yeah.

01:07:52.054 --> 01:07:54.977
And there have to be,
you know, firm and swift consequences

01:07:55.044 --> 01:07:57.788
when we push too hard
against those guardrails.

01:07:57.855 --> 01:08:01.421
You don't have that in Memphis.
You've got kids acting like grown folks.

01:08:01.488 --> 01:08:05.880
I think the largest driver of violent crime
in this city currently is probably kids.

01:08:06.510 --> 01:08:08.216
You know, 12 to 16 years old.

01:08:08.720 --> 01:08:10.955
That's a scary prospect for a teacher.

01:08:11.720 --> 01:08:15.060
Society needs to get back to a meritocracy

01:08:15.127 --> 01:08:18.116
where we move forward
based on the merits of our actions.

01:08:18.183 --> 01:08:21.278
I don't feel entitled
to special treatment because I'm White

01:08:21.345 --> 01:08:23.227
or because I'm an addict in recovery.

01:08:23.294 --> 01:08:28.459
And I think that's how a lot of how
we operate as a society got off-kilter

01:08:28.526 --> 01:08:31.197
is because
we started paying more attention to

01:08:31.264 --> 01:08:34.243
what makes somebody different
rather than exceptional.

01:08:34.310 --> 01:08:37.557
We need to get back to
rewarding exceptionalism.

01:08:38.010 --> 01:08:41.831
And push each other to become the best
version of ourselves that we can be.

01:08:41.898 --> 01:08:44.663
That's the only way
any of this gets any better.

01:08:47.523 --> 01:08:50.639
I do enough out here
that when I lay my head on my pillow,

01:08:50.706 --> 01:08:52.223
I know I couldn't have done more.

01:08:52.290 --> 01:08:55.402
Objectively looking at
all the problems Memphis has,

01:08:55.469 --> 01:08:58.166
it is too much
for one organization to stop.

01:08:58.233 --> 01:09:00.696
It doesn't mean
we haven't had major impact.

01:09:00.763 --> 01:09:03.143
It doesn't mean
that we haven't done great things.

01:09:03.210 --> 01:09:06.733
But I know the problems here
are far too big for just us to tackle.

01:09:06.800 --> 01:09:09.768
They're far too big
for just law enforcement to tackle.

01:09:09.835 --> 01:09:11.885
Which is why we leaned on the White House,

01:09:11.952 --> 01:09:13.862
leaned on the Memphis Safe Task Force.

01:09:13.929 --> 01:09:17.837
So now we do have the playing field
leveled more than we did.

01:09:18.350 --> 01:09:23.360
So now I think if the right people
can lean on the Department of Education,

01:09:23.427 --> 01:09:28.333
on HUD, Health and Human services
to get all the other angles attacked.

01:09:28.400 --> 01:09:31.012
Memphis could have
a renaissance inside three years.

01:09:31.079 --> 01:09:35.262
We've already gone from the deadliest city
in America to off the top ten list

01:09:35.329 --> 01:09:36.649
in a matter of months.

01:09:37.432 --> 01:09:41.482
But the second these feds leave Memphis
all that sh*t's coming right back

01:09:41.847 --> 01:09:44.452
-I haven't seen any feds though yet at all.

01:09:44.519 --> 01:09:46.496
-Have you seen Highway Patrol?

01:09:46.901 --> 01:09:49.601
-What's that?
-Have you seen Tennessee Highway Patrol?

01:09:49.668 --> 01:09:51.744
-Yeah.
-They're riding with them.

01:09:51.811 --> 01:09:52.694
-Okay.
-Yeah.

01:09:52.761 --> 01:09:56.180
Wait til something pops off,
you'll see all of 'em.

01:09:56.247 --> 01:09:58.197
-40 of them.
-You'll see 40 roll up, dude.

01:09:58.264 --> 01:10:00.460
The response time is insane.

01:10:00.988 --> 01:10:02.664
In a good way right now.

01:10:02.731 --> 01:10:07.104
The overwhelming response,
shock and awe factor is a big deal too.

01:10:07.608 --> 01:10:11.007
Hell, they'll even have National Guard
show up for d*mn shoplifting.

01:10:11.074 --> 01:10:12.899
It's crazy. It's a Band-Aid.

01:10:12.966 --> 01:10:15.634
-But if you're saying
these kids are graduating school

01:10:15.701 --> 01:10:17.995
a lot of them not even being able to read.

01:10:18.560 --> 01:10:22.057
Then I mean common sense says…

01:10:22.124 --> 01:10:23.951
It's not gonna get better.

01:10:24.018 --> 01:10:25.862
It's not gonna get better, you know?

01:10:25.929 --> 01:10:28.640
You have to tackle that metric right there.

01:10:28.707 --> 01:10:34.295
I have to make young men in this hood
more likely to be in college or have a job

01:10:34.736 --> 01:10:36.786
than they are to be dead or incarcerated.

01:10:36.853 --> 01:10:39.778
Right now it's the flip of that,
we have to turn that around.

01:10:39.845 --> 01:10:43.272
I think to turn that around it's gonna take
tackling the school system,

01:10:43.339 --> 01:10:48.905
tackling just a whole host of human
problems that are far too great for me.

01:10:48.972 --> 01:10:51.589
Like I'm good at
helping people out of addiction.

01:10:52.209 --> 01:10:53.545
That's kinda my thing.

01:10:54.541 --> 01:10:59.486
I'm not good at helping people
graduate school reading on grade level.

01:10:59.830 --> 01:11:02.123
We need a lot more dumped into this city.

01:11:02.190 --> 01:11:04.790
People don't have any idea
how bad this place is.

01:11:04.857 --> 01:11:06.116
None whatsoever.

01:11:06.183 --> 01:11:09.471
If I bring people from Memphis
to where we live in Georgia

01:11:09.538 --> 01:11:13.844
they're blown away and they think
Forsythe County, Georgia is a unicorn.

01:11:14.536 --> 01:11:16.976
Whereas in reality Memphis is the unicorn.

01:11:17.413 --> 01:11:20.145
This place is unlike
anywhere else I've ever lived

01:11:20.212 --> 01:11:22.752
and I've lived in 28 cities and 14 states.

01:11:23.161 --> 01:11:26.053
This place is very different
from the rest of America.

01:11:26.120 --> 01:11:27.970
That's the dichotomy that is Memphis.

01:11:28.417 --> 01:11:31.358
It feels fine, it feels safe,
all the people are cool.

01:11:32.081 --> 01:11:33.881
-But we're…
-Until you piss them off.

01:11:33.947 --> 01:11:35.627
Until they burn your house down.

01:11:35.694 --> 01:11:38.008
Until they come burn your house down, yeah.

01:11:38.075 --> 01:11:39.755
-They're quick to react.
-Yeah.

01:11:40.460 --> 01:11:43.601
-…I would too
-Yeah, that's a tear down.

01:11:43.668 --> 01:11:45.612
[Peter] When did this thing get burnt?

01:11:45.679 --> 01:11:47.035
[man] About a month or two.

01:11:47.102 --> 01:11:48.105
Month or two?

01:11:48.172 --> 01:11:50.222
-It was cold when that place burned down.

01:11:50.289 --> 01:11:51.242
Three months.

01:11:51.309 --> 01:11:54.405
So had to scare you guys
seeing this go up in flames.

01:11:54.472 --> 01:11:55.785
-Yeah, y'all lucky.

01:11:55.852 --> 01:11:59.127
Got up to go to the bathroom,
it was cold as hell.

01:11:59.194 --> 01:12:01.235
You could feel the heat like summertime.

01:12:01.302 --> 01:12:02.722
-Ooh boy.

01:12:02.789 --> 01:12:03.846
It burnt for a while.

01:12:03.913 --> 01:12:05.440
-Gotta go all the way down.

01:12:05.507 --> 01:12:07.757
-Was it your old neighbor
who set it on fire?

01:12:07.824 --> 01:12:10.555
-It had been vacant a while.
-It had been vacant?

01:12:10.622 --> 01:12:11.505
Yeah.

01:12:11.830 --> 01:12:13.843
How's everything in the hood otherwise?

01:12:13.910 --> 01:12:16.178
Otherwise normal, you know, sh*t happens.

01:12:16.245 --> 01:12:18.952
-But a lot of stuff's
getting built up around here.

01:12:19.019 --> 01:12:21.887
-It ain't bad as people put on to be.

01:12:21.954 --> 01:12:23.827
It's just a normal hood

01:12:23.894 --> 01:12:26.904
-You a waiter
or you go around with the apron?

01:12:26.971 --> 01:12:29.517
-I work at the Rendezvous, I'm a waiter.
-Nice.

01:12:29.584 --> 01:12:30.934
[Ben] Oh, hell yeah.

01:12:31.001 --> 01:12:34.453
Next time we need to take you
to Rendezvous, that's great barbecue.

01:12:34.520 --> 01:12:36.823
They kinda blow it up with the crime thing.

01:12:36.890 --> 01:12:38.236
Blow it out of proportion.

01:12:38.303 --> 01:12:40.224
You feel okay walking around at night?

01:12:40.291 --> 01:12:44.120
Without a doubt.
I actually used to run 15, 20 laps.

01:12:44.187 --> 01:12:45.744
-Down the center.
-Oh, yeah?

01:12:45.811 --> 01:12:49.429
Yeah, I used to run 20 laps
down the center back and forth.

01:12:49.848 --> 01:12:52.854
-You got Junior doing that?

01:12:52.921 --> 01:12:55.074
I stay in German Town,
this my old home.

01:12:55.141 --> 01:12:57.560
I got another home now
I work out in my garage.

01:12:57.935 --> 01:13:00.106
-German Town?
-Yeah.

01:13:00.173 --> 01:13:01.853
-Are you German?
-Not at all.

01:13:02.246 --> 01:13:03.212
[laughter]

01:13:03.279 --> 01:13:04.183
How's it out there?

01:13:04.250 --> 01:13:07.985
I feel like it's the same as here.
No issues.

01:13:08.052 --> 01:13:09.558
-It's just East Memphis now.

01:13:09.625 --> 01:13:11.583
Right, it's just East Memphis.

01:13:11.650 --> 01:13:14.005
-How many kids you got?
-This is my mom and son.

01:13:14.072 --> 01:13:14.955
Okay.

01:13:15.022 --> 01:13:16.231
Two sons and a daughter.

01:13:16.297 --> 01:13:18.609
-Mom, you're looking fresh.
-Mom's staying here.

01:13:18.676 --> 01:13:19.593
D*mn.

01:13:19.660 --> 01:13:21.347
-All right, man.
-All the best.

01:13:21.414 --> 01:13:23.703
Come on down to see us at Rendezvous.

01:13:23.770 --> 01:13:26.033
-Appreciate you.
-Thank you, appreciate you.

01:13:28.422 --> 01:13:32.211
-That's generally the kind of interaction
you will have with Memphis people.

01:13:32.278 --> 01:13:34.014
They're good people in the end.

01:13:34.081 --> 01:13:35.513
Just really good people.

01:13:35.580 --> 01:13:39.565
Some have gone down a bad path
and made a few bad choices.

01:13:39.632 --> 01:13:42.545
There are some actual bad people here
but for the most part

01:13:42.612 --> 01:13:44.048
these are good d*mn people.

01:13:44.115 --> 01:13:46.852
-People come up to you,
the smile, they want to talk.

01:13:47.764 --> 01:13:49.889
There's no attitude, you're not from here.

01:13:49.956 --> 01:13:51.281
-No.
-No, never.

01:13:51.348 --> 01:13:54.430
You'll never get treated that way.
You're very welcomed here.

01:13:54.497 --> 01:13:56.244
-That's true.
-Everywhere I've gone.

01:13:56.311 --> 01:13:58.430
That' comes from
being an underdog I think.

01:13:58.497 --> 01:14:00.665
-I think so too.
-Having a bad reputation.

01:14:00.732 --> 01:14:01.901
There's humility there.

01:14:01.969 --> 01:14:05.311
A sense of humility in the city
that I've never seen anywhere else.

01:14:05.378 --> 01:14:09.189
Everybody's not standing around thinking
they're better than anybody else.

01:14:09.264 --> 01:14:12.662
Everybody's cool with everybody
and that's why I love Memphis.

01:14:12.729 --> 01:14:15.053
-Everybody's in a struggle together here.

01:14:15.120 --> 01:14:18.021
There is an us versus them
mentality sometimes

01:14:18.483 --> 01:14:20.210
but never at a street level.

01:14:20.277 --> 01:14:23.275
On social media, in the news it's always…

01:14:23.342 --> 01:14:26.673
And a lot of it goes back to race,
I'm not gonna dance around that,

01:14:26.740 --> 01:14:28.610
in media. and on social media.

01:14:28.677 --> 01:14:30.519
At a street level it doesn't exist.

01:14:31.063 --> 01:14:33.947
-You think the media pumps that up?
-Hell yeah, they do.

01:14:34.014 --> 01:14:35.319
I'll tell you why.

01:14:36.061 --> 01:14:38.163
Media makes their money from ad revenue.

01:14:38.230 --> 01:14:40.348
Ad revenue is driven by web traffic.

01:14:40.415 --> 01:14:43.255
And I run a few publisher sites,
I know how they operate.

01:14:43.322 --> 01:14:47.489
anything that generates controversy,
gets people arguing, sharing, pissed off,

01:14:47.556 --> 01:14:49.348
texting it to mama, screenshot it,

01:14:49.415 --> 01:14:51.257
that's how you make money in media.

01:14:51.686 --> 01:14:55.424
The news outlets, we've created
a self fulfilling prophecy actually

01:14:55.491 --> 01:14:59.308
because we're incapable of
not engaging with things

01:14:59.375 --> 01:15:01.270
that we see online that piss us off.

01:15:02.007 --> 01:15:05.211
What we're doing when we engage
is we're feeding the beast

01:15:05.278 --> 01:15:07.917
and telling that outlet
run more content like this.

01:15:07.984 --> 01:15:08.887
-Right.

01:15:09.947 --> 01:15:12.615
They look at the analytics, like,
oh, that did great.

01:15:12.682 --> 01:15:15.992
Yeah, and invariably
it's the things that get people fighting

01:15:16.059 --> 01:15:17.876
that generate the most revenue.

01:15:18.453 --> 01:15:22.360
Which sucks because it makes everybody
think we're supposed to hate each other

01:15:22.427 --> 01:15:24.936
but in reality, my buddy Scott, man,

01:15:25.003 --> 01:15:29.730
I love the way he pronounces this,
the digitally re-presented world.

01:15:29.797 --> 01:15:31.442
Not represented, re-presented.

01:15:31.509 --> 01:15:33.968
-Oh, yeah.
-You get it?

01:15:34.035 --> 01:15:38.907
I love the way he does that and it's
so true because we're being given a

01:15:39.674 --> 01:15:42.214
re-presentation of reality.

01:15:42.281 --> 01:15:43.166
Yep.

01:15:43.233 --> 01:15:46.769
Political ideology will collapse
on first contact with human suffering.

01:15:47.215 --> 01:15:51.795
And the more you get out on the streets
and engage with other human beings

01:15:51.862 --> 01:15:55.183
who are suffering in the same fight
you are, you're gonna see that.

01:15:55.250 --> 01:15:57.555
That is your typical Memphis interaction.

01:15:57.622 --> 01:15:58.958
People are just good people

01:15:59.025 --> 01:16:02.649
They're not asking
"Why you filming me and my mom?"

01:16:02.716 --> 01:16:04.686
-Yeah, right.
-They're just talking.

01:16:04.753 --> 01:16:07.418
If anyone looks suspect it's me.

01:16:07.858 --> 01:16:08.840
[Ben laughs]

01:16:08.907 --> 01:16:10.138
-Right?
-Or me.

01:16:11.005 --> 01:16:13.163
-Oh, here we go, this is Graceland.
-Yeah.

01:16:13.649 --> 01:16:17.080
So this is his airplane,
they got a concert venue over here.

01:16:17.510 --> 01:16:18.798
This is his house.

01:16:18.865 --> 01:16:21.765
-That's Elvis' house? No way!
-That's Elvis' house.

01:16:22.502 --> 01:16:24.449
Look at the quality of the road here.

01:16:24.919 --> 01:16:28.590
-Okay, so Elvis was living in the hood
but it wasn't the hood at the time.

01:16:28.657 --> 01:16:30.888
Correct, this was working class back then.

01:16:30.955 --> 01:16:32.218
-How you doing, man?

01:16:32.620 --> 01:16:34.893
See y'alls vlog so I wanted to get in it.

01:16:34.960 --> 01:16:36.749
Yeah, what's going on in the hood?

01:16:38.432 --> 01:16:39.448
All good?

01:16:39.515 --> 01:16:41.743
I ain't from the hood,
I'm from the suburbs.

01:16:41.810 --> 01:16:42.890
Do you love it?

01:16:42.957 --> 01:16:44.526
-It's okay.
-It's cool?

01:16:44.593 --> 01:16:46.189
-Race ya. 
-[giggling]

01:16:48.189 --> 01:16:49.389
[Ben] Turbo Diesel.

01:16:51.072 --> 01:16:54.203
-Nice, holy sh*t! This thing's got juice.

01:16:54.270 --> 01:16:55.153
It does.

01:17:00.456 --> 01:17:02.290
So we're at your warehouse.

01:17:02.357 --> 01:17:06.033
Yeah, this is our warehouse
and I don't want to steal Jess' thunder

01:17:06.100 --> 01:17:09.387
So I'm going to let her give you
the background on what it is today.

01:17:09.454 --> 01:17:12.915
I opened this place
as a job skills training thing.

01:17:12.982 --> 01:17:17.257
We were gonna teach vets and dudes
coming off the streets woodworking.

01:17:17.711 --> 01:17:20.027
So we started this brand
making cutting boards.

01:17:20.094 --> 01:17:22.858
We had our Afghan daughter
working here, dope boys…

01:17:22.925 --> 01:17:26.023
-Afghan daughter?!
-Yeah. We adopted a girl from Afghanistan.

01:17:26.090 --> 01:17:28.249
-During the collapse we got her here.
-Nice.

01:17:28.316 --> 01:17:30.357
-Were you in the military?
-Very briefly.

01:17:30.424 --> 01:17:34.657
But we worked the Afghan evacuation
from '21, we ran 68 safe houses there.

01:17:35.527 --> 01:17:37.112
-No way.
-Yeah, it was wild.

01:17:37.179 --> 01:17:39.893
-A whole 'nother video.
-Whole 'nother video, yeah.

01:17:39.960 --> 01:17:41.893
-Hood Mart?
-Hood Mart, baby.

01:17:42.336 --> 01:17:44.231
[Jess] What you're seeing right here

01:17:44.790 --> 01:17:47.941
started in my towel closet
when I was in active addiction.

01:17:48.008 --> 01:17:50.939
Ben told you before
that we had multiple businesses

01:17:51.006 --> 01:17:52.904
while we were getting high.

01:17:52.971 --> 01:17:55.430
We had an addiction
but also a lot of money.

01:17:55.497 --> 01:17:59.743
And I've always wanted
to help people my entire life.

01:17:59.810 --> 01:18:03.553
So when I was in active addiction
in my closet I would take a bunch of money

01:18:03.620 --> 01:18:06.459
get deodorant,
shampoo, soaps, anything I could,

01:18:06.533 --> 01:18:09.261
and I'd have
a whole little Walmart in my closet.

01:18:09.328 --> 01:18:14.844
So when we went down to South Memphis
to see our drug buddies and prostitutes

01:18:15.370 --> 01:18:17.923
I would bring my stuff and give it away.

01:18:17.990 --> 01:18:21.044
Then we went homeless, lost everything.

01:18:21.428 --> 01:18:26.201
So it's always been my dream
to have a place that's like Goodwill

01:18:26.640 --> 01:18:29.015
but nobody has to pay for anything.

01:18:29.082 --> 01:18:30.558
As stupid as that sounds.

01:18:30.625 --> 01:18:31.973
And that's what this place is.

01:18:32.040 --> 01:18:36.239
You're seeing all this wood right here
because when we first opened it

01:18:36.702 --> 01:18:40.909
Ben's business partner, Robert,
he is a woodworking guy.

01:18:40.976 --> 01:18:43.409
He loves working with wood.
He's very good at it.

01:18:43.476 --> 01:18:45.983
So we would have men
that were unemployable.

01:18:46.050 --> 01:18:49.861
They had felonies,
couldn’t get hired, just out of prison,

01:18:49.928 --> 01:18:53.634
we would bring them here
and let them make cutting boards.

01:18:54.254 --> 01:18:56.214
And so they would get a skill

01:18:56.281 --> 01:18:58.344
They would have a paycheck.

01:18:58.830 --> 01:19:00.516
That since has kinda…

01:19:01.430 --> 01:19:04.043
you can only keep that going for so long.

01:19:04.110 --> 01:19:07.926
When the woodworking shop
kinda went away, we just…

01:19:07.993 --> 01:19:10.213
-Oh, wow.
-This used to be all I had.

01:19:10.280 --> 01:19:11.783
All that was donated.

01:19:11.850 --> 01:19:17.192
When you have nothing and you've
been homeless and you finally get better

01:19:17.259 --> 01:19:19.355
and finally get an apartment.

01:19:19.422 --> 01:19:22.591
you have nothing to put in it
and nothing for your kids.

01:19:23.085 --> 01:19:25.306
You almost want to go get high again.

01:19:25.373 --> 01:19:27.089
Like there's no hope.

01:19:27.156 --> 01:19:28.855
So this is a place of hope.

01:19:28.922 --> 01:19:32.037
This is a place where kids
can pick out the toys they want

01:19:32.104 --> 01:19:36.455
and mothers and fathers
can get shoes and clothes

01:19:36.522 --> 01:19:40.039
to go to the interview to get the job
to keep the money coming in.

01:19:40.106 --> 01:19:42.909
And these are things
a lot of people don't think about.

01:19:42.976 --> 01:19:46.902
Normal people, they're like, go get
some interview clothes, go get a job.

01:19:46.969 --> 01:19:51.312
Sometimes you don't have interview clothes
and don't have two pennies to go get 'em.

01:19:51.379 --> 01:19:52.688
So this is…

01:19:53.353 --> 01:19:56.945
And like this even,
some women have never had this.

01:19:57.012 --> 01:19:59.718
Some women have
never been able to feel beautiful

01:20:00.652 --> 01:20:03.550
and to have clothes
that they consider fancy.

01:20:03.617 --> 01:20:07.159
So that's another thing too.
I don't know if we have them anymore.

01:20:07.226 --> 01:20:10.237
But we used to have
prom dresses up here for girls that

01:20:10.989 --> 01:20:13.146
mama didn't have money
to buy them a dress.

01:20:13.213 --> 01:20:14.332
That's a huge thing.

01:20:14.399 --> 01:20:19.182
So we try to have every single thing
that anybody could ever want

01:20:19.646 --> 01:20:22.627
and we just give it for free
because we're not paying for it

01:20:22.695 --> 01:20:24.695
and I'm not gonna make you pay for it.

01:20:26.281 --> 01:20:27.753
[Peter] All right guys, that was awesome.

01:20:27.820 --> 01:20:30.691
Love what you're doing.
Anything else you want to say?

01:20:30.758 --> 01:20:33.495
-We covered a lot of ground today.
-Lot of ground.

01:20:33.562 --> 01:20:35.633
Didn't even get to see the half of it.

01:20:35.700 --> 01:20:39.733
We were pretty candid with you
about some challenges we were facing.

01:20:39.800 --> 01:20:41.175
But the take away, Peter,

01:20:41.689 --> 01:20:45.964
is that if we can't
get the wrap around part of this right.

01:20:46.869 --> 01:20:49.784
All the ground we've gained
is just gonna be lost again.

01:20:49.851 --> 01:20:53.763
We have to bring
the education system back around.

01:20:53.830 --> 01:20:55.962
We have to bring things like reentry,

01:20:57.138 --> 01:21:01.110
job skills training, economic opportunity,
parenting classes.

01:21:01.631 --> 01:21:05.828
We gotta shift the mentality in Memphis
and if we can't get all of that done,

01:21:06.285 --> 01:21:09.224
then what have we really accomplished?

01:21:10.502 --> 01:21:14.370
Let me say that in a different way,
we've made a big impact in a lot of lives.

01:21:14.437 --> 01:21:17.197
I don't want to take away from that
and if you ask them

01:21:17.264 --> 01:21:20.099
we've changed their entire world
and that's a great thing.

01:21:20.166 --> 01:21:22.664
But I didn't set out to change
one person's world.

01:21:22.731 --> 01:21:25.257
I set out to change an entire city
and to do that

01:21:25.823 --> 01:21:29.163
I guess this is my way of acknowledging
we still got a long way to go

01:21:29.230 --> 01:21:30.846
and I got a lot to figure out.

01:21:30.913 --> 01:21:33.523
-It's awesome you guys.
It comes from a good place.

01:21:33.590 --> 01:21:35.491
Place of passion and purpose

01:21:35.558 --> 01:21:39.984
and guys, I want to let you know
We Fight Monsters, link is down below.

01:21:40.051 --> 01:21:41.763
They got a great website.

01:21:41.830 --> 01:21:44.746
-If you want to be part of this…
-Check our YouTube out too.

01:21:44.813 --> 01:21:47.742
Check YouTube out,
they got all sorts of great content.

01:21:47.809 --> 01:21:50.501
If you feel like giving,
that link is down below.

01:21:50.568 --> 01:21:52.723
Guys, thanks for that awesome day.

01:21:52.790 --> 01:21:55.340
It's a great window
and this is how we learn.

01:21:55.407 --> 01:21:56.931
-Exactly right, brother.
-Yeah.

01:21:56.998 --> 01:21:59.639
And thank you guys
for coming on that journey.

01:21:59.706 --> 01:22:00.906
Until the next one.

01:22:00.973 --> 01:22:02.827
[jazz music]

